        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented to the emergency department via EMS after being a restrained passenger in a T-bone motor vehicle accident at approximately 60 mph. Airbags deployed, but he denied loss of consciousness and was ambulatory at the scene. He initially complained of left hand pain but later developed a headache and left neck pain. He admitted to significant alcohol consumption but denied other complaints.\nObjective: The exam was notable for ecchymosis and edema of the left second and third metacarpals and erythema on the right shoulder. No cervical spinal tenderness was found. Neurological exam was intact with full strength. Blood ethanol level was 70 mg/dL. CT scans of the head and cervical spine, as well as a chest X-ray, showed no acute abnormalities. A left hand X-ray was concerning for an avulsion fracture of the second metacarpal.\nAssessment: Alcohol intoxication, headache, left hand fracture, musculoskeletal neck pain, and MVA.\nPlan: The left hand was immobilized in a radial gutter splint. The patient received ketorolac, acetaminophen, diphenhydramine, and metoclopramide in the ED and was prescribed cyclobenzaprine and ketorolac. He was instructed to follow up with orthopedics in 2-3 days and was discharged in stable condition.",
