        "medicineType": "Emergency Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient was a 34-year-old male who presented to the emergency department via ambulance after being involved as a front passenger in a high-speed motor vehicle collision (MVC) at approximately 60 mph, with moderate front passenger damage and airbag deployment; he was wearing a seatbelt and did not require extrication at the scene. He reported left hand pain as a significant symptom. He denied tobacco, alcohol, and substance use. Numeric pain assessments throughout his ED stay consistently rated his pain as 7 out of 10, localized to the head.\n\nObjective: On EMS arrival, he was awake with stable vital signs (BP 151/92 mmHg, HR 115, RR 18, O2 sat 98%) and a patent airway. Trauma assessments repeatedly documented a patent airway, spontaneous breathing, no uncontrolled bleeding, and clear breath sounds in all lung fields. He was immobilized with C-spine precautions. Neurologically, he was alert and oriented with a Glasgow Coma Score of 15, brisk and equal pupillary responses (pupil size 3 mm initially, later 4 mm), and no focal deficits. Head, face, and eye exams were unremarkable except for left hand pain and neck tenderness on palpation. Chest, abdominal, pelvic, and extremity exams were otherwise normal except for extremity tenderness; all pulses were 2+ and normal, and sensation and strength were intact in all extremities. No abnormalities were found on inspection or palpation of the posterior surfaces. Vitals remained stable throughout the ED stay.\n\nAssessment: The patient was assessed as a trauma activation (Trauma Level II) due to high-speed MVC with left hand pain and neck tenderness but no evidence of major injury, neurological deficit, or hemodynamic instability.\n\nPlan: He was instructed to follow up with orthopedics in 2-3 days, use Flexeril and Toradol as needed for pain, and return to the ED for any new or worsening symptoms. No trauma procedures were performed after initial immobilization.",
