        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented to the ED following a motor vehicle accident, with orders referencing head and neck trauma. He consistently reported pain in the head and left hand, rated 7/10 throughout his stay. He denied alcohol, tobacco, or substance use.\n\nObjective: The patient was hemodynamically stable, though with intermittent mild hypertension (systolic up to 159 mmHg). Neurovascular status was intact distal to injury. Neurological exams were consistently normal, with a GCS of 15, equal and reactive pupils, and full strength and sensation in all extremities. Lungs were clear and heart sounds were normal. An ECG showed normal sinus rhythm. \n\nAssessment: The patient was managed as a Level II trauma case. No acute cardiopulmonary or neurological findings were identified. \n\nPlan: A cervical collar was applied and the patient was made NPO except for medications. A comprehensive workup including labs (CBC, BMP, hepatic panel, troponin, drug/alcohol screen) and imaging (CT head/C-spine, X-rays of chest and left hand) was ordered. Medications administered included acetaminophen, ketorolac (IM), diphenhydramine, metoclopramide, and cyclobenzaprine. Discharge prescriptions were provided for ketorolac and cyclobenzaprine.",
