        "bodyPart": "Head, Face, Hand, Shoulder, Neck, Chest, Abdomen, Back, Stomach, Arm",
        "medicineType": "Internal Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for an ER follow-up three days after a motor vehicle accident on 12/07/2024. He reported feeling \"hit by a train\" with stomach pain, left hand pain, and left shoulder pain, all described as focal and non-radiating. He also noted neck weakness, a feeling of head heaviness, and left arm weakness significant enough that he was unable to open cans. He mentioned new-onset congestion and post-nasal drip since the accident. ER imaging (chest X-ray, CT neck, hand X-ray) showed no fractures.\nObjective: Examination revealed tenderness to palpation of the left maxillary facial and left nasal bone, along with congested nasal turbinates. Neurological exam was grossly normal with 5/5 strength in all extremities.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included MVA, acute left shoulder pain, nasal sinus congestion, left facial pain, and bruising.\nPlan: The patient was started on naproxen for pain, advised to increase cyclobenzaprine, and referred to physical therapy for 12 sessions over 6 weeks. An orthopedics referral was made, and a follow-up was scheduled in two days.",
        "pdfLink": "pdf",
