        "bodyPart": "Hand",
        "medicineType": "Orthopedic",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for a new patient evaluation four days after a motor vehicle accident on 12/07/24, reporting a left hand injury. He complained of pain at the base of his left index and ring fingers, with associated bruising and swelling. The patient noted his left index finger gets cold and wakes him at night. He denied numbness or tingling. He reported taking Flexeril and naproxen, stating Tylenol and ibuprofen provided no relief. He also mentioned his prescribed splint had fallen apart. Past medical history included asthma and sickle cell-hemoglobin Lepore disease.\nObjective: Physical examination of the left hand revealed mild edema without erythema, ecchymosis, or deformity. There was tenderness to palpation over the dorsal hand at the second metacarpal. Range of motion and strength of the left hand and wrist were normal, and the neurovascular exam was unremarkable. A review of x-rays from another facility showed a 'tiny density along ulnar aspect of the second MCP joint,' which appeared to be chronic.\nAssessment: Left hand strain and left hand pain.\nPlan: The patient was advised to avoid heavy lifting, pushing, or pulling but encouraged to continue range of motion exercises. He was instructed to continue using ice, ibuprofen, and Tylenol. Follow-up was recommended in 2-4 weeks if pain persisted.",
