        "facility": "NOMS Fremont Orthopaedics",
        "bodyPart": "Shoulder",
        "medicineType": "Orthopedic",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: A 34-year-old right-handed truck driver presented with left shoulder pain that began after a motor vehicle accident in December 2024. He reported no improvement despite physical therapy and a home exercise program. He described diffuse pain in the shoulder that was worse with movement and weight-bearing, waking him at night. He denied numbness, tingling, popping, or grinding.\nObjective: Examination of the left shoulder was normal on inspection. Palpation revealed mild tenderness anteriorly, posteriorly, and laterally, with moderate tenderness over the bicipital groove and proximal biceps. Range of motion was normal but painful, with active forward elevation to 150 degrees and abduction to 140 degrees. Strength was 5/5 but limited by pain. Special tests were positive for Neer's, painful arc, and Speed's signs. An X-ray of the left shoulder showed no acute bony process.\nAssessment: Biceps tendinitis, acute pain, and impingement of the left shoulder.\nPlan: The patient elected to receive a biceps tendon sheath injection of 40 mg Depo-Medrol. He was advised to follow up in 3-4 weeks, with consideration for a subacromial injection or an MRI if pain persisted.",
