        "provider": "Grant T. Olsen, NP",
        "facility": "NOMS Fremont Orthopaedics",
        "bodyPart": "Shoulder",
        "medicineType": "Orthopedic",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient, a 34-year-old right-handed truck driver, reported persistent left shoulder pain since a motor vehicle accident in December 2024, which had not improved with prior physical therapy or a biceps tendon sheath injection administered on 7/14/2025. He described diffuse pain in the anterior, lateral, posterior, and top of the shoulder, worsened by movement and weight-bearing, and waking him at night; he denied numbness, tingling, popping, or grinding. He reported no relief from the prior injection or physical therapy, and was using acetaminophen, ice, and heat for symptom management. Objective: On 8/4/2025, physical exam revealed normal right shoulder inspection without ecchymosis, edema, atrophy, or masses; tenderness was present over the anterior shoulder and AC joint, with moderate tenderness at the bicipital groove and lateral arm. Right shoulder range of motion was normal but painful, with active forward elevation and abduction to 140 degrees (pain past 90 degrees); strength was 4/5 for abduction and biceps (limited by pain), and 5/5 for triceps. Neurovascular exam was intact, with normal radial pulse, capillary refill, and axillary nerve sensation; scapular position was normal without winging. Special tests were positive for Neer's, Hawkins, painful arc, and Speed's tests, and negative for drop arm and biceps/labral signs. Assessment: The diagnoses were internal derangement of the left shoulder and acute pain of the left shoulder. Plan: The patient was counseled on treatment options and risks, and after discussion, received a 40mg Depomedrol injection to the left shoulder, which he tolerated well; neurovascular status was intact post-injection, and post-injection care instructions were reviewed. An MRI of the left shoulder without contrast was ordered to further evaluate for labral and biceps tendon tear. The patient was instructed to seek urgent evaluation if symptoms worsened or new symptoms developed, and questions were answered in laymen terms at the bedside.",
        "pdfLink": "pdf",
        "painScore": 7,
        "billingAmount": 4800.0,
        "severity": "critical",
