        "summary": "Subjective: The patient, a 34-year-old truck driver, presented for an evaluation of left shoulder pain stemming from a December 2024 motor vehicle accident, which had not improved. He reported diffuse pain and noted no relief from two prior injections or physical therapy.\nObjective: Examination revealed moderate tenderness over the anterior shoulder and lateral arm. Range of motion was normal but painful. Special tests were positive for painful arc, O'Brien's test, and clicking/popping.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included internal derangement and acute pain of the left shoulder. An MRI from 08/08/25 revealed a possible tiny nondisplaced tear of the anterior inferior labrum with an intact rotator cuff.\nPlan: The MRI findings were reviewed with the patient. Due to the possible labral tear, he was referred to Dr. Stepanic for further evaluation. A home exercise program handout was provided.",
