        "provider": "Mark Khalil, MD; Alexandra Prosser, MD",
        "facility": "Family Health Services",
        "bodyPart": "Genitals, Shoulder, Arm",
        "medicineType": "Internal Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for evaluation of new hematospermia, reporting two painless episodes of blood in his ejaculate following prolonged abstinence and multiple ejaculations with a new partner. He denied penile discharge, dysuria, testicular pain, or systemic symptoms. He also noted unrelated post-operative left shoulder pain at 7-8/10. His surgical history included a left rotator cuff repair.\nObjective: The patient was alert and in no acute distress. Genital and scrotal exams were normal. His left upper extremity was in a fixed sling. Blood pressure was elevated at 143/94 mmHg, with a repeat reading of 167/99 mmHg.\nAssessment: Diagnoses were hematospermia, screening for sexually transmitted diseases, and obesity (BMI 31.07).\nPlan: In-office urinalysis was normal. A full STI panel was ordered. The patient was reassured about the likely benign nature of the symptoms and advised to maintain consistent, non-traumatic sexual activity. Follow-up was scheduled in one month to review lab results. Cyclobenzaprine was discontinued, while oxycodone was continued as needed.",
        "pdfLink": "pdf",
        "painScore": 8,
        "billingAmount": 4800.0,
