        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "First encounter (01/15/2026, Physical Therapy): The patient presented for physical therapy following left shoulder arthroscopy, reporting left shoulder pain, decreased range of motion (ROM), and weakness causing increased difficulty with activities of daily living (ADLs), self-care, sleep, and decreased quality of life. On exam, left shoulder passive ROM was flexion to 100, abduction to 80, external rotation to 10, internal rotation to 30, and extension to 0; active ROM was external rotation to 5 and flexion to 70, with strength at least 3+/5 at the elbow but not formally assessed at the shoulder due to post-op protocol. Treatment included 12 minutes of manual therapy (PROM, gentle massage, stretching), 29 minutes of therapeutic exercise (ROM, flexibility, strength as allowed), and 10 minutes of cold pack post-session with electrical stimulation as needed. The patient demonstrated good tolerance to exercise, with continued active assistive and added active ROM in supine with little challenge, and home exercise program (HEP) was reviewed and decreased to 1-2 times per day. PROM was progressing well with minimal to moderate restriction in all planes, and the plan was to continue as tolerated. Short-term goals included achieving pain-free left shoulder active ROM and strength, clearance to return to work full duty pending physician approval, and independence with HEP at discharge from PT. Pain rating was not documented.\nSecond encounter (01/12/2026, Physical Therapy): The patient, a 35-year-old truck driver, presented with left shoulder pain following a motor vehicle accident approximately one year prior to surgery on 12/1/25, with current deficits of pain, weakness, and decreased ROM/flexibility. He reported no pain on arrival, having just come from an orthopedic appointment where he was cleared to discharge his sling, begin active assistive movement, and return to light duty at work. Aggravating factors included prolonged sling use, movement, ADLs/self-care, and position changes, while rest and ice were relieving. Post-operative precautions included no strengthening until approximately 12 weeks unless cleared sooner by the physician. On exam, left shoulder PROM was flexion to 100, abduction to 80, external rotation to 10, internal rotation to 30, and extension to 0; active ROM was external rotation to 5 and flexion to 70, with strength at least 3+/5 at the elbow but not formally assessed at the shoulder due to post-op protocol. Treatment included 12 minutes of manual therapy (PROM, gentle massage, stretching), 32 minutes of therapeutic exercise (ROM, flexibility, strength as allowed), and 10 minutes of cold pack post-session with electrical stimulation as needed. The patient demonstrated good tolerance to exercise, added active assistive ROM in standing and supine with minimal difficulty, and completed stretching at a doorway, all pain-free, which were added to his HEP. PROM was progressing well with minimal to moderate restriction in all planes, and the plan was to continue as tolerated. Short-term goals were the same as above. Pain rating was not documented.",
