        "summary": "First encounter (01/22/2026, Physical Therapy): The patient, Nathan R Caldwell, a 35-year-old male, presented to physical therapy with a chief complaint of left shoulder pain following a motor vehicle accident approximately one year prior to left shoulder arthroscopy on 12/1/25. He reported current deficits of pain, weakness, and decreased range of motion/flexibility, with minimal pain upon arrival, located in the left shoulder and upper arm. Aggravating factors included prolonged sling use, movement, activities of daily living/self-care, and position changes, while rest and ice provided relief. He worked as a truck driver with some manual labor and climbing duties. Post-operative precautions included no strengthening until approximately 12 weeks unless cleared by his physician due to recent SLAP/Bankart repair. On exam, left shoulder passive range of motion was flexion to 100\u00b0, abduction to 80\u00b0, external rotation to 10\u00b0, internal rotation to 30\u00b0, and extension to 0\u00b0; active range of motion was external rotation to 5\u00b0 and flexion to 70\u00b0, with strength at least 3+/5 at the elbow but not formally assessed at the shoulder due to post-op protocol. Treatment included 10 minutes of manual therapy (PROM, gentle massage, stretching), 13 minutes of therapeutic exercise (ROM, flexibility, strength as allowed), and cold pack post-session with electrical stimulation as needed. The patient demonstrated good tolerance to exercise, with progress in flexion, moderate restriction in abduction, and minimal end-range pain in external rotation; he continued to have left shoulder pain, decreased ROM, and weakness impacting ADLs, sleep, and quality of life. Short-term goals included achieving pain-free left shoulder AROM and strength \u22654/5 in all planes.\n\nSecond encounter (01/29/2026, Physical Therapy): The patient continued to report left shoulder pain, decreased ROM, and weakness causing increased difficulty with ADLs/self-care, sleep, and decreased quality of life. Short-term goals remained focused on achieving pain-free left shoulder AROM and strength \u22654/5 in all planes, clearance to return to work full duty pending physician approval, and independence with a home exercise program for maintenance/progression at discharge from PT.",
