        "summary": "Subjective: The patient was seen for follow-up after left shoulder arthroscopy performed approximately six weeks prior, reporting minimal discomfort and improvement in pain, with continued use of an ultra sling and ongoing physical therapy; he described a recent episode of increased pain after a friend accidentally bumped his arm, which was managed with ibuprofen and ice, denied numbness or tingling, and noted difficulty sleeping comfortably, often sleeping in a recliner, with no drainage and well-healing incisions after suture removal on 1/12/2026. Objective: On 1/22/2026, physical exam of the left shoulder revealed no tenderness, active abduction to 100 degrees, forward flexion to 140 degrees, normal muscle strength at 4+/5, absence of erythema, presence of healed portal site scars, normal sensation, and present pulse. Assessment: The diagnosis was status post arthroscopy of the left shoulder (ICD-10 Z98.890). Plan: The patient was educated on risks of lifting, pushing, and pulling with the affected arm, and initially was unable to return to work with light duty as he is a commercial driver, but was subsequently cleared to return to work with no restrictions at this time, with follow-up planned in four weeks; he was advised to seek emergency care if symptoms worsened or new symptoms developed. Medications included albuterol inhaler, erythromycin gel, esomeprazole, fexofenadine, and fluticasone nasal spray. Allergies to fluticasone-salmeterol, ibuprofen, and lorazepam were noted, all causing hives. No imaging or procedures were ordered during the 1/22/2026 encounter.",
