        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "First encounter (03/05/2026, Physical Therapy): Nathan R Caldwell, a 35-year-old male, presented to physical therapy with a chief complaint of left shoulder pain following a motor vehicle accident (MVA) approximately one year prior to left shoulder arthroscopy on 12/1/25. He reported current deficits of pain, weakness, and decreased range of motion/flexibility, but denied pain upon arrival and noted that his pain at night was improving. Pain was localized to the left shoulder and upper arm, aggravated by prolonged sling use, movement, activities of daily living/self-care, and position changes, and relieved by rest and ice. He worked as a truck driver with some manual labor and climbing. On exam, left shoulder passive range of motion (PROM) was flexion to 100, abduction to 80, external rotation to 10, internal rotation to 30, and extension to 0; active range of motion (AROM) was external rotation to 5 and flexion to 70; strength was at least 3+/5 at the elbow but not formally assessed at the shoulder due to post-op protocol. Treatment included 10 minutes of manual therapy (PROM, gentle massage, gentle stretching), 32 minutes of therapeutic exercise (ROM, flexibility, strength as allowed), and modalities were declined; cold pack was applied post-session and electrical stimulation unit (ESU) was to be added as needed. He was cleared for strengthening, with progression of exercises and addition of 1 lb weight with supine and side-lying active range of motion, and tolerated therapy well with only muscle fatigue and no pain reported that day. Short-term goals included achieving pain-free left shoulder AROM and strength, return to work full duty pending physician approval, and independence with home exercise program.\n\nSecond encounter (02/19/2026, Physical Therapy): Nathan R Caldwell presented to physical therapy with left shoulder pain, weakness, and decreased ROM/flexibility following the same MVA and subsequent arthroscopy. He denied pain upon arrival and reported improvement in night pain. Exam findings were similar to the later visit, with PROM flexion to 100, abduction to 80, external rotation to 10, internal rotation to 30, extension to 0; AROM external rotation to 5, flexion to 70; and elbow strength at least 3+/5, not formally assessed at the shoulder. He was under precautions due to SLAP/Bankart repair, with no strengthening until 12 weeks post-op unless cleared by his physician. Treatment included 10 minutes of manual therapy, 30 minutes of therapeutic exercise, and modalities were declined; cold pack was applied post-session and ESU was to be added as needed. He demonstrated good tolerance to therapy, with PROM progressing well and minimal restriction in all planes, and was to return to duty on 2/23/26.",
