        "medicineType": "Emergency Medicine",
        "recordType": "Emergency Department Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented to the emergency department after a motor vehicle accident that morning where he struck another vehicle while driving approximately 45 mph. He complained of pain in the right ankle, right knee, left shoulder, and lower back. He denied head trauma, chest pain, shortness of breath, nausea, vomiting, or focal neurologic deficits. Relevant history included hypertension, morbid obesity, and tobacco use.\nObjective: The patient was alert and oriented with stable vital signs. Examination revealed tenderness to palpation of the right knee, right ankle, left shoulder, and midline lumbar spine, but no deformities or step-offs were noted. CT of the lumbar spine showed no acute fracture, but revealed mild facet arthropathy, a suspected disc bulge at L4-5, and possible neural foraminal stenosis at L4-5 and L5-S1, with a recommendation for MRI for better evaluation. An x-ray of the right ankle revealed a tiny fracture of the dorsal aspect of the navicular bone. A right knee x-ray was negative for acute fracture but noted a chronic ununited anterior tibial tubercle. A left shoulder x-ray showed no acute osseous abnormality.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included closed navicular fracture of the right foot, right knee sprain, left shoulder sprain, and right ankle sprain.\nPlan: The patient was treated with ibuprofen, provided a post-op shoe and crutches, and instructed on RICE (rest, ice, compression, elevation) therapy. He was prescribed lisinopril for hypertension and varenicline for smoking cessation and was advised to follow up with Industrial Health in three days. The patient received and acknowledged understanding of educational materials regarding his injuries and home care instructions.",
