        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented to the emergency department after a high-speed motor vehicle collision (MVC) at work, arriving by private vehicle and reporting right leg pain rated as high as 9/10 on a numeric pain scale upon arrival, which gradually improved to 6/10 by the end of the ED stay. The patient denied alcohol use and substance abuse, but was a current every day smoker. Objective: On arrival, the patient was awake, alert, and oriented with a Glasgow Coma Score of 15 throughout the encounter. Airway was patent, breathing was spontaneous, and there was no uncontrolled bleeding. Breath sounds were clear bilaterally, heart sounds were normal, and vital signs were stable except for a mildly elevated pulse (111 bpm). Pupils were equal and reactive, skin was pink, and capillary refill was less than 3 seconds. There were no abnormalities on inspection or palpation of the head, face, neck, chest, abdomen, pelvis, or perineum. Tenderness was noted on palpation of the right leg, left shoulder, and back, but there were no deformities, and strength and sensation were intact in all extremities. No trauma procedures or blood transfusions were performed. Assessment: The patient was evaluated as a trauma level II due to high-speed MVC with isolated musculoskeletal tenderness and no evidence of head, neck, chest, abdominal, or pelvic injury. Plan: The patient was monitored with serial trauma assessments and vital signs, with pain scores improving from 9/10 to 6/10 during the ED stay; no procedures or imaging were documented, and the patient was discharged the same day.",
