        "summary": "Subjective: The patient recounted a 04/12/23 incident where the semi-truck he was driving collided head-on, injuring his left shoulder, low back, and right leg, knee, and ankle. As of this visit, he reported constant low back pain unrelieved by lidocaine patches, improving right knee pain for which he was in physical therapy, and was now wearing an ASO brace on his right foot instead of a boot. He described his low back pain as worsening through the day to 7-8/10, making his legs and feet feel cold and tingly, but improving when lying down. He expressed frustration with the lack of improvement in his back pain six weeks post-injury.\nObjective: The patient appeared in moderate distress. Examination of the lumbar spine revealed midline and right-sided tenderness, with flexion limited to 45 degrees and extension to 30 degrees. A straight leg raise test on the right was negative but caused low back pain at 30 degrees. Neurological exam was normal.\nAssessment: Acute lumbar myofascial strain with radiculopathy; displaced navicular fracture of the right foot; and sprains of the right ankle, right knee, and left shoulder. The lumbar strain was not improving, while the other injuries were. The patient had a history of morbid obesity and hypertension.\nPlan: The patient was kept off work due to inability to sit or stand for any length of time. He was started on gabapentin 300 mg twice daily, advised to continue meloxicam and ice, and a lumbar spine MRI and physical therapy were ordered. Follow-up was scheduled in two weeks.",
