        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported ongoing right foot pain from a navicular fracture, with pain increasing to 7-8/10 after physical therapy despite wearing an ASO brace. He also reported lower back pain at 8/10, described as dull, throbbing, and radiating down both legs with numbness and tingling, which worsened with sitting and walking. The patient expressed frustration with the lack of improvement in his back pain six weeks post-injury. He noted his knee and shoulder pain had resolved.\nObjective: The patient was full weight-bearing on the right foot. A lumbar spine MRI from 06/07/2023 showed multilevel degenerative changes, most prominent at L4-L5 and L5-S1 with focal disc herniations. Specific findings included a central posterior disc herniation with mild to moderate spinal canal stenosis at L4-L5, and a posterior disc herniation greater to the left with left-sided neural foraminal stenosis at L5-S1. Scout images noted a possible issue at T11-T12 requiring further evaluation if warranted.\nAssessment: Lower back strain and right foot navicular fracture. Right knee and left shoulder pain resolved.\nPlan: The patient was to continue wearing the ASO brace and follow up with Dr. Pacos. He continued taking Mobic, Aleve, and gabapentin.",
