        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up on his workers' compensation claim, reporting he had a recent lumbar MRI and a physical therapy session. He recounted the 04/12/23 MVA and initial injuries. As of 05/19/23, his shoulder and knee pain had resolved, but lower back pain persisted at 8/10 with radiation down both legs, numbness, and tingling, which was worse with sitting and walking. Right foot pain was 4-5/10. He stated gabapentin helped his pain somewhat.\nObjective: The patient was in mild distress and obese. Exam showed tenderness of the lumbar spine radiating bilaterally, pain with range of motion, and a positive straight leg raise test causing pain into the buttock. A 06/07/23 lumbar MRI showed multilevel degenerative changes with focal disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1, causing mild to moderate spinal canal stenosis at L4-L5.\nAssessment: Acute lumbar myofascial strain, displaced right foot navicular fracture, and sprains of the right ankle, right knee, and left shoulder.\nPlan: Gabapentin was increased to 600 mg BID, tizanidine 4 mg BID PRN was prescribed, and physical therapy for the lumbar spine was approved.",
