        "medicineType": "Physical Therapy",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "On 6/9/2023, the patient presented for physical therapy following a right navicular fracture and right knee strain sustained on 4/12/23, reporting lateral ankle pain in the area of the ATFL after discontinuing a boot and transitioning to an ASO brace, as well as persistent left-sided low back pain; the patient stated that MRI and PT orders had been written but authorization for low back treatment had not yet been received, and reported an MRI on 6/7/23 showing bulging discs and herniations from L3 to S1. Pain was rated 3-4/10 in the lateral ankle (improving) and 6-7/10 in the low back (worse with sitting). Exam showed pain with deep pressure to the ATFL, ankle weakness, abnormal gait with lack of pushoff and heel strike, and more pronounced low back pain than ankle pain. Interventions included 20 minutes of therapeutic exercise, 15 minutes of manual therapy to the ankle, and 10 minutes of neuromuscular re-education. The plan noted minimal pain to the lateral right ankle (inferior to the lateral malleoli, improving), minimal sway with tandem stance, and low back pain with weight bearing; the patient was educated on core stabilization and McKenzie extension exercises, and a low back evaluation was scheduled for 6/13/23. On 6/5/2023, the patient reported similar complaints with pain rated 3-4/10 in the lateral ankle (improving) and 7-8/10 in the low back (worse with sitting), and exam findings and interventions were similar; the patient was again educated on core stabilization and McKenzie extension exercises, and an ice pack was placed on the low back during bike exercise with pain reduction noted last session, but the patient had forgotten to do the McKenzie exercises at home and was reminded to continue them.",
