        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for an initial physical therapy evaluation for lower back pain and right ankle/foot injuries from a work-related MVA on 04/12/23. Back pain began the same day, rated 5-9/10, radiating down the back of his legs to his feet, with numbness in the lower legs if sitting too long. He reported back pain was the main obstacle to returning to work. A 06/07/23 MRI reportedly showed bulging discs from L2-S1. Medications included Tylenol, Meloxicam, and Gabapentin.\nObjective: The patient was obese with loss of lumbar lordosis. Examination revealed lumbar flexion limited to 50% with pain, pain with side bending, hypomobility, and hypersensitivity to touch throughout the lumbar spine. Straight leg raise was positive bilaterally. The Back Index score indicated 58% disability.\nAssessment: Diagnosis included loss of lumbar range of motion, spinal mobility, and poor tolerance to sitting and activity due to pain, with good rehab potential.\nPlan: Physical therapy was planned for three times weekly for four weeks. Treatment involved manual therapy, McKenzie exercises, and electrical stimulation.",
