        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "First encounter (6/13/2023, Physical Therapy): The patient presented for physical therapy following a motor vehicle accident on 4/12/23, in which he sustained a right navicular fracture and right ankle sprain, and reported that back pain began the same day as the accident and was now his most significant barrier to returning to work, with constant pain rated 7-8/10 in the low back that worsened with activity and improved upon waking; he also reported pain radiating down both legs, especially with sitting, and inquired about a back brace for stabilization. MRI on 6/7/23 showed bulging discs from L2 through S1. Physical therapy interventions included manual therapy, McKenzie extension exercises, and electrical stimulation/cryotherapy for pain control; the patient reported decreased pain to 4/10 and improved posture after the session. The plan was for PT three times per week for four weeks.\n\nSecond encounter (6/12/2023, Physical Therapy Evaluation): The patient described the mechanism of injury as a semi truck pulling out in front of him while working for R &amp; L Transportation, resulting in a right navicular fracture, right ankle sprain, and onset of back pain the same day, with pain rated 5-9/10, constant, radiating from the center of the low back down the legs to the feet, and associated with numbness in the lower legs if sitting too long; he denied any prior similar back pain. Symptoms were aggravated by sitting, lifting, and walking, and improved with lying flat, Tylenol, Meloxicam, Gabapentin, and ice. Objective findings included lack of lumbar lordosis, limited lumbar flexion to 50% with pain, pain with side bending, hypersensitivity to touch throughout the lumbar spine (especially lower segments), and hypomobility on joint play, but no neurological deficits. Special tests were positive for straight leg raise bilaterally and pain with passive knee-to-chest, with better tolerance of massage than mobilizations. Assessment noted loss of lumbar ROM, poor tolerance to sitting and activity, and 58% disability on Back Index; short-term goals included pain reduction, improved lumbar mobility, and return to light duty work. The plan was for PT three times per week for four weeks, with interventions including manual therapy, McKenzie exercises, HEP, and education on activity modification.",
