        "medicineType": "Occupational Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported ongoing right foot pain with inward rotation, minimal swelling, and constant lumbar pain, stating physical therapy had not helped and he was not working. He had been released by his orthopedist and was no longer wearing a boot, but still had right foot/ankle pain (2-3/10, burning) and back pain (6/10, up to 8/10) that radiated down the left leg. He reported that PT had not improved his condition. The patient has a history of hypertension, morbid obesity, and smoking.\nObjective: The patient appeared in moderate distress, holding his back. Examination revealed tenderness across the low back, worse on the left, and into the left buttock. Straight leg raise was positive on the left. There was mild edema and tenderness on the right distal lateral malleolus and dorsal foot. Right ankle range of motion was full but painful with inversion and eversion.\nAssessment: Displaced fracture of the navicular of the right foot; sprains of the right knee, right ankle, and shoulder; and acute lumbar myofascial strain. A motion was submitted to add diagnoses of L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniations and L4-L5 spinal canal stenosis based on a 06/07/23 MRI.\nPlan: The patient was to continue Meloxicam, Gabapentin, and Tizanidine. Referrals were made to podiatry, neurosurgery, and pain management, and an MRI of the right ankle was ordered. He was restricted to light duty work for 4 hours/day, with a sitting limit of 30-60 minutes and no lifting. Follow-up was scheduled in 4 weeks.",
