        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported ongoing pain in the right foot with minimal swelling and constant low back pain which had not improved with physical therapy. He noted the initial shoulder and knee pain had resolved, but he continued to have right foot/ankle pain at 2-3/10 and severe low back pain at 6-8/10 radiating into the left buttock and down the lateral leg.\nObjective: Examination revealed tenderness across the low back, worse on the left, and into the left buttock. Lumbar range of motion was limited, and a straight leg raise was positive on the left. The right ankle had mild edema and tenderness. A 06/07/23 lumbar spine MRI showed multilevel degenerative changes with focal disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1, resulting in mild to moderate spinal canal stenosis at L4-L5 and left neural foraminal stenosis at L5-S1.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included right navicular fracture, knee and ankle sprains, and acute lumbar myofascial strain. A motion was submitted to add diagnoses for the L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniations and spinal stenosis based on MRI findings.\nPlan: Medications included Meloxicam, Gabapentin, and Tizanidine. The patient was released from orthopedic care for his foot and ankle but was unable to return to truck driving. He was approved for light duty work with restrictions of 4-hour days, no lifting, and frequent breaks. Referrals were made for a right ankle MRI, podiatry, neurosurgery, and pain management. A follow-up was scheduled in 4 weeks.",
