        "bodyPart": "Ankle, Knee, Back, Lower Back",
        "medicineType": "Physical Therapy",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "First encounter (07/14/2023, Physical Therapy): The patient presented with persistent right lateral ankle pain following a right navicular fracture and right knee strain sustained on 4/12/23, reporting pain rated 4-5/10 that remained constant and did not worsen with activity, and expressed frustration about lack of progress and ongoing pain despite working light duty for 4 hours per day in a sit/stand role. The patient limped into physical therapy, described his ankle as 'messed up,' and demonstrated pain on passive and active inversion but not with other motions, with intermittent limping during gait and normal muscle strength and range of motion except for pain at end range inversion. Swelling was noted around the lateral malleolus, which was unusual for the patient and attributed to increased activity over the past two days; the patient did not wear supportive shoes or a brace and was insistent that something more was wrong with his ankle, leading the therapist to recommend discontinuing PT due to plateaued progress and to await MRI results. Interventions included manual therapy, ultrasound, electrical stimulation, and therapeutic exercise.\n\nSecond encounter (07/12/2023, Physical Therapy): The patient reported low back pain that began the same day as the accident (4/12/23) in which he sustained a right navicular fracture and right ankle sprain, with MRI showing bulging discs from L2 through S1 and focal disc herniations with extrusions at L4-L5 and L5-S1. The patient stated that back pain was the main barrier to returning to work, describing it as constant, central, and rated 4-5/10, with inability to sit for more than one hour and no leg symptoms at that time. Referrals for pain management, surgery, and further MRI were being made, and the patient was working 4 hours per day light duty with restrictions on sitting and driving.",
