        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "First encounter (07/14/2023, Physical Therapy): The patient presented with persistent right lateral ankle pain rated 4-5/10, reporting frustration about ongoing pain and lack of progress following a right navicular fracture and right knee strain sustained on 4/12/23, with pain localized to the lateral ankle and not worsening with activity; the patient limped into physical therapy and described his ankle as 'messed up'. Pain was elicited only on passive and active inversion, with all other ankle motions pain-free, and intermittent limping was observed during gait. Swelling was noted around the lateral malleolus, which was attributed to increased activity over the past two days, and the area was tender to palpation at the ATFL; the patient did not wear supportive shoes or a brace and insisted something more was wrong with his ankle, though no excessive inversion range of motion was found, but pain persisted at end range. Interventions included manual therapy, posterior talar mobilizations, pulsed ultrasound, electrical stimulation, and therapeutic exercise; however, the therapist recommended discontinuing physical therapy due to plateaued progress and was awaiting MRI approval.\n\nSecond encounter (07/12/2023, Physical Therapy): The patient reported low back pain (central, not radiating to the legs, rated 4-5/10) that began the same day as the accident on 4/12/23, which also resulted in a right navicular fracture and right ankle sprain; the back pain was described as constant and most interfering with his ability to return to work, as he could not sit for any length of time and requested light duty with restrictions on sitting and driving. MRI from 6/7/23 showed multilevel degenerative changes, most prominent at L4-L5 and L5-S1, with focal disc herniations and extrusions. The plan included referrals for pain management, surgery, and further MRI, as well as a home exercise program focused on extension.",
