        "date": "07/18/2023",
        "provider": "Matthew D. McDaniel, MD",
        "facility": "Quest Evaluations",
        "bodyPart": "Ankle, Shoulder, Knee, Foot, Back, Lower Back",
        "medicineType": "Orthopedic",
        "recordType": "Independent Medical Examination",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported he was injured on 04/12/2023 in a motor vehicle collision where his semi was totaled, leading to medical treatment for a right ankle sprain, left shoulder sprain, right knee sprain, low back strain, and a displaced dorsum navicular fracture of the right foot. He described ongoing pain on the outside of his right foot and ankle with instability, and constant low back pain radiating down his left leg to the foot. The patient denied any prior similar injuries. His past medical history included hypertension, and his medications were lisinopril, gabapentin, meloxicam, and an unknown muscle relaxant.\nObjective: Initial emergency department x-rays showed no acute findings. A lumbar MRI on 04/18/2023 revealed multi-level disc space narrowing, with a posterior disc herniation with an extruded component at L4-5, and a posterior disc herniation with an extruded disc extending into the left neuroforamen at L5-S1. On examination, the left shoulder and right knee were normal. The right ankle showed significant lateral swelling, tenderness, and suggested instability with reduced motion. The lumbosacral spine exam revealed paraspinal muscle hypertonicity and spasm, reduced range of motion, decreased strength in the left EHL and plantar flexion, decreased sensation in the left S1 distribution, and a positive left straight leg raise test.\nAssessment: The left shoulder sprain, right knee sprain, and displaced dorsum navicular fracture of the right foot were deemed resolved and at Maximum Medical Improvement. The right ankle sprain and low back strain were considered clinically evident and not at Maximum Medical Improvement.\nPlan: The patient was working light duty, and current work restrictions were considered appropriate. Recommendations included an MRI of the right foot and ankle and a spine surgery evaluation due to significant ongoing clinical findings.",
