        "facility": "Fisher-Titus Medical Center",
        "bodyPart": "Ankle, Foot",
        "medicineType": "Radiology",
        "recordType": "Imaging Report",
        "summary": "Body Part: Right ankle and right foot MRI without contrast. Impression: The MRI demonstrated no acute osseous findings, a chronic appearing deformity of the dorsal navicular, diffuse muscle atrophy especially of the plantar midfoot, and longitudinal split type tearing involving the peroneus brevis. The patient had a history of right foot pain following a motor vehicle accident in April, with continued right lateral foot and ankle pain. The MRI technique included routine imaging of the right ankle/hindfoot and right forefoot, with comparison to radiographs from 4/12/2023. Cartilage of the tibiotalar, subtalar, and midfoot joints appeared preserved; talofibular, tibiofibular, calcaneofibular, and deltoid ligaments were intact; mild distal Achilles tendinosis was noted without tear; extensor and medial flexor tendons were intact; and there was no significant tendon retraction of the peroneus brevis. Additional findings included physiologic joint fluid, no evidence for acute fracture, osteochondral lesion, or osteomyelitis, and normal plantar aponeurosis, sinus tarsi, and tarsal tunnel. In the right foot, bone marrow signal was normal, subcutaneous tissues were unremarkable, there were mild degenerative changes of the first MTP joint without significant effusion, flexor and extensor tendons and plantar plates were intact, there was no evidence of Morton's neuroma, the Lisfranc ligament was intact, and muscle atrophy was again noted. No other significant abnormality was identified.",
