        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up of injuries sustained in a 04/12/23 motor vehicle accident. He reported ongoing right ankle pain at 2-3/10, increasing to 4-5/10 with activity. He also reported low back pain at 6/10 which radiated into his left buttock and down the left leg to his foot. He complained of associated numbness and tingling down the left leg and felt best when lying down. He noted he had completed physical therapy but did not feel it provided improvement.\nObjective: Physical examination revealed a limping gait and tenderness across the low back. Lumbar spine range of motion was flexion to 70 degrees, extension to 30 degrees, and lateral bends to 25 degrees. Sensory and motor function were normal. A prior MRI of the right ankle on 08/03/23 showed a chronic deformity of the dorsal navicular, diffuse muscle atrophy, and longitudinal split tearing of the peroneus brevis. A prior lumbar spine MRI from 06/07/23 showed multilevel degenerative changes with focal disc herniations, most prominent at L4-L5 and L5-S1, resulting in mild to moderate central canal stenosis at L4-L5 and left neural foraminal stenosis at L5-S1.\nAssessment: Acute lumbar myofascial strain, unspecified sprains of the shoulder, right knee, and right ankle, and displaced fracture of the navicular in the right foot. Past medical history included hypertension, morbid obesity, and smoking.\nPlan: The patient was placed on light duty work restrictions for 4 hours/day, 20 hours/week, with no truck driving or lifting. Medications including meloxicam, tizanidine, and gabapentin were continued and refilled. Follow-up was scheduled with Dr. Dolce and Dr. Blades, and a neurosurgery consult was planned.",
