        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for evaluation of a work-related right ankle injury sustained in a motor vehicle accident on 04/12/2023 when he \"rear-ended another car\". He reported that initial radiographs showed no fracture or dislocation. Despite conservative therapies including immobilization, physical therapy, and bracing, he continued to have significant lateral ankle pain rated 8/10. The patient denied any previous issues with his foot or ankle. He also reported a history of work-related lower back issues affecting his left lower extremity.\nObjective: Physical examination of the right ankle revealed mild circumferential swelling at the lateral malleolus. There was pain with resisted inversion and eversion of the ankle at the peroneal brevis tendon and exquisite tenderness at the watershed region. Muscle strength was 5/5 in all tested muscles. Vascular and neurological exams were intact.\nAssessment: Peroneal tendon rupture, right; Other synovitis and tenosynovitis, right ankle and foot.\nPlan: After reviewing imaging and discussing the failure of conservative care, the provider recommended surgical intervention, specifically repair of the longitudinal tear of the peroneal brevis tendon with tubularization and synovectomy. The patient was counseled on the risks and benefits and consented to the procedure. No medication changes were made.",
