        "summary": "Subjective: The patient, a 32-year-old male, presented for evaluation of a right foot and ankle injury from a work-related motor vehicle accident on 04/12/2023 where he rear-ended another car. He reported no prior issues with his foot or ankle. An emergency department visit post-accident revealed no fracture. Despite conservative care including immobilization, physical therapy, and bracing, he continued to have significant pain rated 8/10 at the lateral ankle. A recent MRI showed a longitudinal split tear of the peroneal brevis tendon in the right lateral ankle. He also reported related lower back issues affecting his left lower extremity, for which a lumbar MRI was done on 06/13/2023.\nObjective: Physical examination revealed intact vascular status and sensation. Mild circumferential swelling was noted around the right ankle. There was pain with resisted inversion and eversion of the ankle at the peroneal brevis tendon, mild tenderness over the sinus tarsi, and exquisite tenderness at the watershed region. Range of motion was painless and no tendon subluxation was present.\nAssessment: Peroneal tendon rupture, right, initial encounter; other synovitis and tenosynovitis of the right ankle and foot.\nPlan: After discussing the MRI findings and failure of conservative care, surgical intervention was recommended. The proposed procedure was repair of the longitudinal tear of the peroneal brevis tendon with tubularization and synovectomy. The patient was counseled on risks including pain, infection, nonunion, and CRPS, and consented to the procedure. No new medications were prescribed.",
