        "medicineType": "Cardiology",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for a preoperative cardiovascular evaluation for a planned foot surgery related to an injury from a few months prior. He denied chest pain, palpitations, or dizziness. He reported being active with functional class I prior to the injury. History included hypertension, currently treated with lisinopril 40 mg daily, and being a current smoker (1.5 packs daily).\nObjective: The patient was alert and in no acute distress with a normal physical exam. Vital signs included blood pressures up to 160 systolic, heart rates up to 98, and a BMI of 52.51. An in-office EKG showed nonspecific ST-T changes and mild sinus tachycardia.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included mildly abnormal EKG due to hypertensive heart disease, hypertension, mild sinus tachycardia, morbid obesity, and tobacco use.\nPlan: The provider concluded the patient could proceed with the low-risk surgery from a cardiac standpoint, with an operative risk under 1%. Metoprolol XL 25 mg daily was added for elevated blood pressure and sinus tachycardia. The patient was counseled on metabolic syndrome, smoking cessation, and weight loss.",
