        "medicineType": "Podiatry",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for a postoperative week 1 follow-up after repair of the right peroneal brevis tendon. Pain was reportedly managed with ice, elevation, and medication. The patient denied fevers, chills, nausea, vomiting, calf pain, and shortness of breath and was noted to be relatively compliant with postoperative care. The patient also denied abdominal pain, cramping, loose stool, gastric ulcers, low back pain, knee pain, systemic arthritis, burning, tingling, and transient paralysis.\nObjective: Physical exam revealed palpable DP/PT pulses bilaterally, capillary refill &lt;3 seconds, and intact monofilament and vibratory sensation bilaterally. Muscle strength was +5/5 in all tested muscles, and a Homans test was negative. The surgical site was healing well with minimal swelling, no signs of infection or dehiscence, and intact sutures.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included right peroneal tendon rupture and subluxation of the right ankle joint, both initial encounters.\nPlan: A dry sterile dressing with betadine was applied, and the patient was placed in a multilayer compressive Jones posterior splint. Instructions were given to continue ice and elevation, remain compliant with weight-bearing status, start daily aspirin for DVT prophylaxis, and follow up in one week for suture removal.",
