        "medicineType": "Podiatry",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "First encounter (10/10/2023, Podiatry): The patient presented for a postoperative follow-up two weeks after a right peroneal brevis tendon repair. They denied fevers, chills, nausea, vomiting, calf pain, and shortness of breath, and reported that pain was being managed with ice, elevation, and pain medication; compliance with postoperative care was noted. The patient was taking 81 mg aspirin daily for DVT prophylaxis and had a history of hypertension. On exam, there was positive hair growth to both feet, good skin turgor, no skin openings, palpable pulses, intact capillary refill, intact sensation, normal muscle strength, and no signs of infection or dehiscence at the surgical site, with minimal swelling. The assessment was peroneal tendon rupture, right, initial encounter. Sutures were removed, Steri-Strips and a compressive dressing were applied, and the patient was instructed to remain nonweightbearing and follow up in one week.\nSecond encounter (10/02/2023, Podiatry): The patient presented one week after right peroneal brevis tendon repair and denied fevers, chills, nausea, vomiting, calf pain, and shortness of breath, with pain managed by ice, elevation, and pain medication. The patient was taking similar medications and had a history of hypertension. Exam findings included positive hair growth, good skin turgor, palpable pulses, intact sensation, normal muscle strength, and a healing incision without infection or dehiscence. The assessment included peroneal tendon rupture, right, initial encounter, and subluxation of the right ankle joint, initial encounter. A dry sterile dressing was applied, the patient was placed in a compressive splint, instructed to continue ice and elevation, start aspirin for DVT prophylaxis, and follow up in one week for suture removal..",
