        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back, Shoulder, Leg, Knee, Ankle, Hip, Buttocks, Thigh, Foot, Lower Leg",
        "medicineType": "Orthopedic",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for evaluation of low back pain following a work-related truck accident on 04/12/2023, where another vehicle pulled in front of his semi; he reported injuring his left shoulder, low back, right leg, right knee, and right ankle. He stated he had no back pain prior to this incident. He described significant low back pain extending to the buttocks bilaterally and into the left thigh, calf, and foot, with symptoms worse on the left side. Symptoms worsened with sitting, walking, and bending, but improved with lying down. He recently had right ankle surgery and was using a knee cart. Prior physical therapy was not helpful.\nObjective: The patient had difficulty rising from a seated position. There was significant pain over the left sacral sulcus and a positive Fortin finger test bilaterally. Left sacroiliac joint pain was reproduced with Faber, thrust, and Gaenslen maneuvers. Motor strength was 5/5 in the lower extremities. A 06/07/2023 lumbar MRI showed an L4 central/left paracentral disc herniation and a left foraminal L5 disc herniation causing moderate foraminal narrowing.\nAssessment: Diagnoses were back strain, lumbar herniated disc (M51.26), and bilateral sacroiliitis (left greater than right).\nPlan: Recommended a left sacroiliac joint injection, physical therapy for sacroiliitis, and bilateral hip x-rays with follow-up after imaging.",
