        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for a three-week follow-up after a right peroneal brevis tendon repair. He reported managing pain with ice, elevation, and medication, and being \"relatively compliant\" with postoperative care. He was taking 81 mg of aspirin daily and denied fevers, chills, nausea, vomiting, calf pain, shortness of breath, low back pain, knee pain, or systemic arthritis. He also denied neurological symptoms such as burning or tingling.\nObjective: Physical examination of both feet was unremarkable with positive hair growth, good skin turgor, palpable pulses, and capillary refill under 3 seconds. Neurological and musculoskeletal exams were normal. The surgical incision was healing well with minimal swelling and no signs of infection.\nAssessment: Right peroneal tendon rupture; subluxation of the right ankle joint; strain of muscle, fascia, and tendon of the lower back.\nPlan: The patient was instructed to begin ambulating in a pneumatic walking boot, which was dispensed and fitted during the visit. No changes were made to his medications.",
