        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported follow-up for a work-related motor vehicle accident on 4/12/23, when his semi-truck collided with another semi. He sustained injuries to his left shoulder, low back, right leg, knee, and ankle, but denied head trauma and initially refused ambulance transport. He reported persistent and severe pain in the right ankle (9-10/10), right knee (7-8/10), left shoulder (3-4/10), and low back (5-8/10), with pain radiating down both legs with numbness and tingling. He stated sitting and bending worsened his back pain and that physical therapy provided some help but did not resolve his pain. He was not taking any medications at the time of the visit, stating nothing helped.\nObjective: The patient was in moderate distress. Examination of the lumbar spine revealed tenderness in the left low back into the buttock and hip, with limited and painful range of motion and a positive straight leg raise on the left. Previous imaging included a right ankle x-ray showing a dorsal navicular fracture, a lumbar CT showing possible herniation at L4-L5, and a lumbar MRI showing herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1.\nAssessment: Acute lumbar myofascial strain, lumbar herniated disc, sacroiliitis, and sprains of the shoulder, right knee, and right ankle.\nPlan: The patient was instructed to obtain hip x-rays and follow-up with Dr. Siegal for pain management and neurosurgery. Work restrictions were assigned for a 4-hour day, 5 days per week, with no truck driving, no lifting, and frequent position changes when sitting.",
