        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back, Shoulder, Leg, Knee, Ankle, Foot, Buttocks, Hip",
        "medicineType": "Occupational Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient, a truck driver, reported being involved in a motor vehicle accident on 4/12/23 when another semi pulled out in front of him, resulting in a collision at approximately 30-35 mph and causing significant damage to his truck; he initially refused ambulance transport and did not experience pain at the scene, but developed significant pain in his left shoulder, low back, right leg, right knee, and right ankle as time progressed, with the worst pain in the right ankle (9-10/10), right knee (7-8/10), and low back (5-6/10). He denied head injury, loss of consciousness, or bowel/bladder dysfunction. He was found to have a small dorsal navicular fracture of the right foot on ER x-ray and was placed in a walking boot. He later developed continuous low back pain radiating to both legs, with numbness and tingling, and described the pain as throbbing and burning, worsened by sitting, bending, and walking, and improved by lying down. He also reported persistent right foot/ankle pain, especially with driving, and intermittent improvement in right knee and left shoulder pain, which eventually resolved. He was treated with meloxicam, gabapentin, tizanidine, acetaminophen, and physical therapy, but reported minimal improvement in back pain. He was placed on work restrictions including no truck driving, no lifting, and frequent position changes when sitting, with a 4-hour workday, 5 days per week. He underwent surgical repair of the peroneal brevis tendon of the right foot on 9/28/23 and was later cleared to return to work from a foot standpoint, but continued to require restrictions for back pain. He was evaluated by pain management, and additional diagnoses of lumbar herniated disc and sacroiliitis were requested.\n\nObjective: Examination revealed tenderness in the middle left low back into the left buttock and hip, mild tenderness across the right low back, painful side bending (left greater than right), limited flexion and extension, and positive straight leg raise on the left; the patient was alert, oriented, and in moderate distress. Review of systems was notable for moderate, radiating back pain, numbness, and tingling.\n\nAssessment: Diagnoses included unspecified sprain of the shoulder, right knee, and right ankle, displaced fracture of the right foot navicular, acute lumbar myofascial strain, lumbar herniated disc, and sacroiliitis. Imaging revealed a small dorsal navicular fracture of the right foot, multilevel degenerative changes with focal disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1, and chronic deformity of the dorsal navicular with peroneus brevis tendon tearing.\n\nPlan: The patient was instructed to continue work restrictions for back injury, return to work from a right foot injury standpoint, and follow up with pain management and neurosurgery; he was also instructed to obtain hip x-rays and follow up in two months.",
        "pdfLink": "pdf",
        "painScore": 10,
        "billingAmount": 4800.0,
