        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for an Independent Medical Examination (IME) regarding an injury from 04/12/2023 when his semi-truck struck another trailer. He reported injuring his right ankle, right shoulder, and back, with pain at the top of his buttocks radiating down both legs (worse on the left to the top of the foot). He denied any prior back problems. He stated sitting for more than 1-2 hours was painful, pain worsened throughout the day, and he had difficulty with long walks. He reported being off work since September 2023 and was receiving no current treatment.\nObjective: Examination revealed a slow, wide-based gait, diffuse low back tenderness, and limited lumbar motion. Sensation and reflexes were normal, and straight leg raise testing was negative for radicular pain. A review of a 06/07/2023 MRI noted L4-5 and L5-S1 disc herniations, while an 08/03/2023 foot MRI showed chronic deformity but no acute findings.\nAssessment: The examiner opined the patient has degenerative disc disease and that his physical exam did not clinically support the diagnoses of lumbar disc herniation at L4-5/L5-S1 or sacroiliitis. The examiner concluded that the MRI findings were degenerative and not causally related to the work accident. The allowed condition of low back strain was determined to have reached Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI).\nPlan: The examiner recommended no further formal treatment, only a home exercise program. It was opined that the patient could return to his former job with no restrictions based on the allowed conditions of the claim.",
