        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up regarding injuries from a work-related MVA on 04/12/23, when his semi-truck collided with another, injuring his left shoulder, low back, and right leg/knee/ankle. He reported persistent low back pain radiating down both legs and significant right ankle pain (9-10/10). While his right knee and shoulder pain resolved, the low back pain persisted, reaching up to 9/10. An MRI on 06/07/23 showed multilevel degenerative changes with disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1. He underwent right peroneal brevis tendon repair on 09/28/23 and was cleared by podiatry to return to work from a foot perspective, but continued to have disabling back pain. He stopped taking prescribed medications, stating they provided no relief.\nObjective: Physical examination revealed lumbar paravertebral tenderness, limited flexion to 50 degrees and extension to 20 degrees, and positive bilateral straight leg raise tests. Sensory exam was normal.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included lumbar disc herniation, acute lumbar myofascial strain, and a displaced fracture of the right navicular. Approval for a sacroiliitis diagnosis was pending a hearing.\nPlan: The patient was advised to continue light daily walking and use ice/heat as needed. Work restrictions were set at 4 hours per day with no lifting. Follow-up was scheduled in three months.",
