        "medicineType": "Neurosurgery",
        "recordType": "Operative Report",
        "summary": "Preoperative diagnosis was lumbar disk herniation and other intervertebral disc displacement in the lumbar region. The patient underwent a caudal epidural nerve block with epidural fluorogram and use of fluoroscopy, performed by Dr. Siegal. The procedure involved prepping the back and buttock, local anesthesia with 1% lidocaine, insertion of a 20-gauge spinal needle into the caudal epidural space, and injection of 20 mL of 1% lidocaine and 80 mg of Depo-Medrol, followed by withdrawal of the needle and placement of a Band-Aid; the patient tolerated the procedure well and was transferred to recovery in stable condition. No significant abnormalities were found during the procedure, estimated blood loss was none, no drains or blood products were used, and there were no complications. Postoperative diagnosis remained lumbar disk herniation and other intervertebral disc displacement in the lumbar region. Discharge instructions included gentle activities for the first day, resuming normal activities the following day, minimizing heavy lifting and strenuous exercise, incision care, resuming normal diet with increased fluids, minimizing pain medications to assess procedure effectiveness, and restarting routine medications unless otherwise directed. The patient was instructed to call the surgeon for fever, drainage, redness, warmth at the injection site, calf pain, unusual swelling, shortness of breath, loss of bowel/bladder control, chest pain, or abnormal coughing, and to avoid driving, alcohol, or making legal decisions for 24 hours.",
