        "summary": "Preoperative diagnosis was sacroiliitis, not elsewhere classified, and the patient underwent a left sacroiliac joint injection with image guidance using local anesthesia and no primary closure, drains, or blood products administered. Intraoperatively, the back, buttock, and hip were prepped and draped in sterile fashion, and a 22-gauge spinal needle was inserted percutaneously into the left sacroiliac joint under fluoroscopic guidance; half a mL of Omnipaque 300 was injected to confirm placement, followed by injection of 80 mg Depo-Medrol and 2 mL of 0.5% Marcaine. The patient was allowed to stand, bend, twist, and walk in the procedure room, but experienced 0% relief while anesthetized. Postoperative diagnosis remained sacroiliitis, not elsewhere classified, with no significant abnormalities found, no complications, and the patient was stable and tolerated the procedure well. The patient was transported to recovery in stable and satisfactory condition.",
