        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented as a new patient for medical weight loss, seeking assistance to qualify for spine surgery as recommended by Dr. Siegel. He reported having independently lost 35 pounds over the prior two months by following a \"carnivore diet\". He used CBD and marijuana for back pain but denied any history of drug abuse or binge eating disorders. He requested Adipex due to its affordability. He reported constant, burning, and aching pain in his back and both legs, rated 6/10 at rest and 9/10 with activity.\nObjective: The patient was obese with a BMI of 49.28 kg/m\u00b2 and weight of 203.5 kg (448 lbs), down from 217.7 kg (480 lbs) in October 2024. Blood pressure was 141/90 mmHg. The remainder of the physical exam was unremarkable.\nAssessment: Morbid obesity with BMI of 50.0-59.9, adult. The patient had achieved a weight loss of over 1 pound per week and more than 5% of his baseline weight prior to initiating therapy. No contraindications to weight loss medication were identified.\nPlan: The patient elected to start phentermine for weight loss pending lab results. Labs including a toxicology screen, CBC, CMP, and TSH were ordered. Risks, benefits, and side effects of short-term oral medication were discussed. A nutrition referral was placed, and he was to follow up in one month. Current medications included lisinopril 40 mg, metoprolol succinate 25 mg, and acetaminophen 325 mg as needed.",
