        "bodyPart": "Back",
        "medicineType": "Bariatric Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "First encounter (01/30/2025, Bariatric Clinic): The patient presented for follow-up regarding obesity and gradual weight gain, seeking assistance with weight loss and reporting a 20 lb loss this month, with a goal of 22 lbs for bariatric surgery eligibility. The patient denied side effects such as chest pain, palpitations, headaches, or sleep disturbance, and blood pressure was at goal. He reported dietary changes (eating twice daily, coffee throughout the day, steak and eggs for lunch, protein and vegetables for dinner) and was encouraged to eat three meals daily and meet a calorie goal of 1400-1600 calories. Exercise was a struggle due to back pain, and chair exercises or pool use were recommended. The patient had previously used phentermine for weight loss and denied heart disease. Weight readings from the last three encounters showed a decrease from 217.7 kg (480 lb) on 10/31/24 to 194.2 kg (428 lb 3.2 oz) on 01/30/25, with a current BMI of 47.04 kg/m\u00b2. Review of systems was negative for malaise, significant weight loss, fever, headache, swelling, lumps, goiter, pain, neck swelling, cough, wheezing, shortness of breath, chest pain, leg swelling, palpitations, abdominal discomfort, blood in stools, black stools, change in bowel habit, lesions, rash, itching, sleep disturbance, mood disorder, and psychosocial stressors. Physical exam showed obese appearance, alert and oriented, normal head, ENT, skin, lungs, heart, abdomen, extremities, and psychiatric findings. The plan included medically managed weight loss therapy, dietary and lifestyle changes, continued phentermine, discussion of risks and benefits, a three-month weight loss goal of 22 lbs, review of labs, and follow-up in one month. Medication changes included starting phentermine 37.5 mg daily before breakfast, with previous instructions to start with half a tablet and increase if tolerated after the first week. The medication list at the end of the visit included acetaminophen, lisinopril, metoprolol succinate, and phentermine.\nSecond encounter (02/27/2025, Bariatric Clinic): The patient returned for follow-up regarding obesity, reporting completion of two months of phentermine and a total weight loss of 23 lbs, though only 3 lbs lost this month and increased junk food consumption. He continued to eat 2-3 times daily with smaller portions but poorer choices, reviewed dietary handout, and struggled with exercise due to back pain, with chair exercises and swimming discussed as alternatives. The patient had previously used phentermine for weight loss and denied heart disease. Weight readings from the last three encounters showed a decrease from 203.5 kg (448 lb 9.6 oz) on 01/02/25 to 192.8 kg (425 lb) on 02/27/25, with a current BMI of 47.88 kg/m\u00b2. Review of systems was negative for malaise, significant weight loss, fever, headache, swelling, lumps, goiter, pain, neck swelling, cough, wheezing, shortness of breath, chest pain, leg swelling, palpitations, abdominal discomfort, blood in stools, black stools, change in bowel habit, lesions, rash, itching, sleep disturbance, mood disorder, and psychosocial stressors. Physical exam showed BP 133/85, pulse 96, height 2.007 m (6'7\"), and no abnormal findings noted.",
        "pdfLink": "pdf",
        "painScore": 7,
        "billingAmount": 4800.0,
        "severity": "critical",
        "isPreCrash": false,
        "isStarred": false
