        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back, Leg, Knee, Ankle, Foot, Shoulder, Buttocks",
        "medicineType": "Occupational Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up of his 04/12/23 MVA injuries, reporting chronic low back pain rated 7/10 that radiated down both legs, left worse than right, with associated throbbing, burning, and numbness. He was wearing his back brace. He had undergone a right peroneal brevis tendon repair and was cleared from a podiatry standpoint, but significant back pain persisted. A neurosurgeon, Dr. Seigal, recommended surgery but required the patient to lose weight first due to being a high-risk candidate. The patient was working with a weight loss clinic and had lost 20 lbs since his last visit. He was fired from his job and was unable to work due to pain. He continued to appeal for approval of sacroiliitis and disc herniation diagnoses.\nObjective: Exam revealed tenderness across the lower back, worse on the left, with very limited lumbar range of motion and a slow gait. Previous imaging confirmed a right navicular fracture and lumbar disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1.\nAssessment: Acute lumbar myofascial strain and herniated lumbar disc.\nPlan: The patient was advised to continue weight loss efforts. Lyrica was discontinued and baclofen 10 mg TID PRN was started. He was to continue Tylenol, use his back brace, and remain off work due to pain, with a follow-up scheduled in 6 months.",
