        "date": "05/13/2025",
        "provider": "Joel D. Siegal, MD",
        "facility": "Avita Health System - Galion Hospital",
        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back, Buttocks, Leg, Lower Leg, Thigh",
        "medicineType": "Orthopedic",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up on lumbar spine complaints following a work-related injury, having lost 100 lbs to become a candidate for surgery. He reported bilateral numbness and tingling in his legs, pain at 8/10 (worst 10/10), and used an L0650 brace for stability. He described severe difficulty rising from a seated position, an inability to stand straight, and walking bent forward with a limp due to significant low back pain radiating to his buttocks, posterolateral thighs, and calves bilaterally.\nObjective: Examination revealed significant pain in the bilateral paraspinous and gluteal regions, severe difficulty rising from a chair, and an inability to stand straight, walking bent forward with a limp. Motor strength was intact at 5/5 in the bilateral lower extremities. A prior MRI (06/07/2023) showed L5-S1 degenerative disc disease with moderate disc herniations, a CT scan (10/14/2024) showed no significant facet arthropathy, and lumbar x-rays showed no dynamic instability.\nAssessment: The diagnosis was back strain (S39.012A). The provider opined the patient had not reached maximum medical improvement as surgery was a viable option and that he was a \"very good candidate\" for a two-level total disc arthroplasty (TDA).\nPlan: A new lumbar spine MRI was ordered to confirm the surgical recommendation. The plan was to proceed with a two-level TDA at L4-5 and L5-S1 pending the new MRI results.",
