        "medicineType": "Neurosurgery",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: Patient presented for follow-up on his lumbar spine and evaluation as a candidate for total disc arthroplasty (TDA) at L4-5 and L5-S1, having lost 100 lbs since his last visit. He reported bilateral numbness and tingling in his legs, with pain rated 8/10, worsening with standing, walking, and bending. He was unable to work, had severe difficulty rising from a seated position, and walked bent forward with a limp. He described significant low back pain radiating to his buttocks, posterolateral thighs, and calves bilaterally, with pain reaching 10/10 at its worst.\nObjective: Patient was able to walk on his toes and heels with 5/5 motor strength in his lower extremities. An MRI from 06/07/2023 showed L5-S1 degenerative disc disease with moderate disc herniations. A CT from 10/14/2024 showed no significant facet arthropathy, and lumbar X-rays showed no dynamic instability.\nAssessment: Back strain.\nPlan: A new lumbar spine MRI was ordered to rule out herniated disc with neural compression, as the prior scan was almost two years old. The patient was considered a very good candidate for a 2-level TDA at L4-5 and L5-S1. It was noted that he had not reached maximum medical improvement as a surgical option was available. After discussion of risks and benefits, the patient expressed a desire to proceed with surgery pending the new MRI results.",
