        "medicineType": "Orthopedic",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: Following a work-related injury, the patient developed significant low back pain extending to the buttocks, posterolateral thighs, and calves bilaterally. He rated his pain as 10/10 at its worst. Symptoms reportedly worsened with standing, walking, bending, and activity, and improved slightly with rest. The patient stated he has been unable to work due to his symptoms.\nObjective: The patient's BMI was in the low 40s. A prior CT from 10/14/2024 showed no significant facet arthropathy, a bone density scan was normal, and lumbar spine x-rays showed no signs of dynamic instability.\nAssessment: The provider opined that the patient had not reached maximum medical improvement, as a surgical option with a good success rate was available. The provider considered him a \"very good candidate\" for a 2-level total disc arthroplasty (TDA) due to recent significant weight loss.\nPlan: A new MRI of the lumbar spine was recommended to confirm surgical appropriateness. If confirmed, the plan was to proceed with a 2-level TDA at L4-5 and L5-S1. The patient, after discussing risks and benefits, expressed a desire to proceed with surgery.",
