        "date": "08/01/2025",
        "provider": "Preet M. Gudimella, MD; Jaclynn Grace Missler, FNP-C",
        "facility": "Fisher-Titus Medical Center Family Medicine - New London",
        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back",
        "medicineType": "Family Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for surgical clearance for lumbar disc replacement surgery and reported significant lower back pain due to lumbar disc herniation, primarily at L4 and L5, with pain described as variable in intensity and worsening with activities such as bending and sitting; the patient stated, 'Some days it's real, real bad and some days it's just enough to irritate you,' and noted reduced activity levels due to pain, primarily managing symptoms by lying down, using marijuana, and Tylenol, but expressed financial constraints limiting access to additional therapies such as trigger point injections. The patient also reported muscle cramps and pain running up the back when the pain starts, and a history of significant weight loss and high caffeine intake. The patient denied alcohol and substance abuse but reported smoking at least half a pack of cigarettes daily.\n\nObjective: On exam, the patient was alert, well hydrated, and not in acute cardiopulmonary distress, with a BMI of 43.71, heart rate of 108, and normal respiratory and cardiovascular findings; the depression screen was negative.\n\nAssessment: The patient was cleared for lumbar disc replacement surgery scheduled for 8/11/2025, with chronic, uncontrolled lumbar disc herniation causing significant lower back pain, muscle tightness, and cramps; additional diagnoses included tachycardia (likely due to caffeine and pain), nicotine dependence, and morbid obesity (BMI 40.0-44.9).\n\nPlan: The patient was cleared for surgery, prescribed nicotine patches for smoking cessation, and encouraged to increase hydration, reduce caffeine intake, and consider post-surgical therapies; weight management and dietary counseling were discussed, and the patient was advised to notify the provider if interested in a referral.",
