        "summary": "Subjective: The patient was referred from neurosurgery for a postoperative wound infection after L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc arthroplasty on 08/11/2025. The patient developed persistent drainage from the wound postoperatively. Wound cultures on 09/02/2025 grew Klebsiella aerogenes and Enterococcus faecalis. The patient denied fevers or chills but reported improving anterior and posterior pain at 5/10 with radiating left leg pain. Past medical history was significant for hypertension, anemia, obesity, and daily tobacco use.\nObjective: Physical examination revealed an obese patient, not in acute distress, with an anterior abdominal wound covered by a wound vac, with no tenderness noted. Labs from 08/27/2025 showed elevated WBC (14.4), platelets (421), and CRP (12.6). An x-ray of the lumbar spine on 08/27/2025 showed the disc replacements at L4-5 and L5-S1 with mild degenerative changes.\nAssessment: Superficial postoperative wound infection with concern for possible deeper infection. Other diagnoses included morbid obesity and tobacco use.\nPlan: Doxycycline was discontinued. The patient was started on oral levofloxacin and amoxicillin-clavulanate with a probiotic Abdominal CT, CBC, CMP, ESR, and CRP were ordered. The patient was advised to follow up and seek emergency care for any worsening symptoms.",
