        "medicineType": "Infectious Disease",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for urgent evaluation of a postoperative wound infection following an L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc arthroplasty on 08/11/2025. He reported malodorous, cloudy, and discolored drainage from the wound starting 09/01/2025. A wound culture from 09/02/2025 grew Enterococcus faecalis and Klebsiella aerogenes. The patient reported mild, improving posterior and anterior pain at 5/10 with improving radiation down the left leg. He denied systemic symptoms like fever, chills, sweats, nausea, or vomiting. His history was significant for hypertension, anemia, obesity, and daily tobacco use.\nObjective: The patient was obese but not in acute distress. Vital signs included a temperature of 97.7\u00b0F and a pulse of 109. A wound VAC was in place on the anterior abdominal wound, with no tenderness, swelling, or erythema noted. There was no palpable lumbar spine pain. Labs from 08/27/2025 showed leukocytosis (WBC 14.4), mild anemia (Hgb 12.7), and elevated CRP (12.6). An 08/27/2025 lumbar spine x-ray showed mild degenerative changes and the disc replacements without acute findings.\nAssessment: Superficial postoperative wound infection with concern for a possible deeper infection due to the anterior surgical approach.\nPlan: The patient was instructed to stop taking doxycycline. He was prescribed oral levofloxacin 750 mg daily and amoxicillin-clavulanate 875 mg twice daily, along with a probiotic. Labs (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP) and an abdominal CT were ordered to evaluate for a deeper infection. The patient was advised to go to the ER if symptoms worsened. The case was discussed with the surgeon.",
