        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for a postoperative seroma following an anterior approach disc surgery on 08/11/2025, which was performed for a herniated disc sustained at work on 04/02/2023. He developed a small opening along the incision which became infected, for which he was placed on antibiotics. The wound was improving but continued to drain copiously, and he had been using Prevena negative pressure therapy. He reported his back pain was much improved with minimal discomfort from the abdominal wound.\nObjective: Physical examination revealed an open wound in the midline low abdominal region that was mostly beefy red and healthy with minimal yellow slough. The wound was 4 cm deep with undermining all around and a 7 cm tunnel at the 2 o'clock position. It produced large to copious amounts of serous drainage without odor, redness, or tenderness.\nAssessment: Non-healing surgical wound, subsequent encounter; Herniation of intervertebral disc between L5 and S1.\nPlan: The patient was to continue oral antibiotics. The wound was packed with iodoform gauze and covered with dry dressings. Negative pressure wound therapy was recommended to manage drainage and promote healing due to the wound's depth and tunneling. A request was to be submitted for therapy approval, and the patient was instructed to call for a follow-up visit once supplies were received.",
