        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for chronic wound care following an L5-S1 disc surgery on 08/11/2025, which was performed for a work injury from 04/02/2023. He developed a post-surgical infection along the anterior incision line. He reported his back pain was much improved, but the abdominal wound had minimal discomfort, significant drainage, and the dressing had been accidentally pulled off by his dog. Pain was rated 2/10 in the abdomen/back at rest and 6/10 with activity.\nObjective: The patient was obese but not in acute distress. An open wound was noted in the low midline abdomen, measuring 0.9 cm wide and 3 cm deep, with a 7 cm tunnel at the 2 o'clock position and copious yellow serous drainage.\nAssessment: Non-healing surgical wound; herniation of intervertebral disc between L5 and S1.\nPlan: The wound was packed with iodoform gauze and covered. Negative pressure wound therapy was recommended and a C9 was to be submitted for approval. Several medications were discontinued, including oxycodone-acetaminophen, naproxen, and methocarbamol. Active medications included lisinopril, phentermine, levofloxacin, and amoxicillin-clavulanate.",
