        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back, Abdomen",
        "medicineType": "Certified Nurse Practitioner",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up of a postoperative seroma following an 08/11/2025 anterior disc surgery, which was performed for a work-related herniated disc from 04/02/2023. An initial small opening in the incision drained copious clear fluid, and negative pressure therapy was delayed pending BWC approval. The patient reported minimal drainage and no pain, stating he had not needed to cover the wound for the past two days. He denied redness, odor, or fevers.\nObjective: The patient was obese but not in acute distress. An open wound in the midline of the very low abdomen appeared mostly beefy red and healthy with minimal yellow slough. Exploration revealed a deep tunnel/pocket in the 12-1 o'clock region measuring 9.5 cm deep, which drained copious amounts of clear serous fluid without odor. There was no surrounding redness, tenderness, or induration. The patient was alert and oriented.\nAssessment: Non-healing surgical wound, subsequent encounter (T81.89XD).\nPlan: The wound opening was too small for negative pressure therapy; iodoform packing was initiated, to be changed three times weekly at the wound center. A wound culture was collected from deep within the wound. Dr. Crum recommended the patient go to the ER for a CT scan and IV antibiotics, but the patient declined due to concerns about being billed for it. His antibiotics were adjusted. Follow-up was scheduled in one week.",
