        "medicineType": "Internal Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported ongoing malodorous, cloudy, and discolored drainage from a postoperative wound following an L4-S1 disc arthroplasty on 08/11/2025. Drainage began on 09/01 and worsened, becoming bloody/brownish by 09/22. A wound care clinic evaluated the wound depth at 9 cm. The patient denied systemic symptoms of infection like fever. He declined a recommended ER evaluation for a possible deeper infection.\nObjective: A wound culture from 09/02 grew Enterococcus faecalis and Klebsiella aerogenes. Laboratory results showed elevated CRP (16), ESR (36), and WBC (12.6). No imaging results were available.\nAssessment: Superficial postoperative wound infection with concern for a deeper infection that could track to the surgical site. Other diagnoses included morbid obesity and tobacco use.\nPlan: Prescribed oral antibiotics levofloxacin 750 mg daily and metronidazole 500 mg three times daily for 7 days. Instructed to discontinue doxycycline. A short-term follow-up was recommended. The patient was advised to go to the ER for fever or worsening pain. Despite recommendations for an ER visit for possible IV antibiotics and drainage, the patient deferred.",
