        "medicineType": "Internal Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: Patient presented for follow-up on a postoperative wound infection following L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc arthroplasty from 8/11/25. He reported ongoing bloody/brownish drainage from a 9 cm deep abdominal wound. Drainage had become malodorous, cloudy, and discolored on 9/1, and a culture on 9/2 grew Enterococcus and Klebsiella. The patient denied any systemic symptoms of infection. He had previously deferred a recommended ER evaluation for a CT scan, IV antibiotics, and possible drainage.\nObjective: Labs showed leukocytosis (WBC 12.6), elevated CRP (16), and ESR (36). A 9/2 wound culture grew Klebsiella aerogenes and Enterococcus faecalis, resistant to Augmentin but susceptible to ampicillin, vancomycin, and others.\nAssessment: Superficial postoperative wound infection with concern for a deeper infection that could track to the surgical site and affect mobility. The provider noted issues with patient compliance and insurance.\nPlan: Levaquin 750 mg daily and Augmentin 875 mg twice daily were prescribed, and doxycycline was discontinued. A repeat wound culture was taken. The patient was instructed to return in one week or seek emergency care if symptoms worsened. The case was discussed with the surgeon and wound care clinic.",
