        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up of a postoperative seroma/wound infection after L5-S1 and L4-L5 disc arthroplasty on 08/11/2025, which followed a work injury on 04/02/2023. He reported ongoing, malodorous, cloudy drainage from the abdominal wound since 9/1, but denied fever, nausea, or vomiting. He reported some mild, improving posterior and anterior pain at 5/10 with radiation down the left leg, as well as constant, cramping back pain rated 7/10 at rest and 10/10 with activity. A wound culture on 9/2 had shown Enterococcus faecalis and Klebsiella aerogenes.\nObjective: The patient was not in acute distress. Vitals included BP 157/100 and pulse 93. An open wound was present on the lower midline abdomen, which on exploration revealed a 9.5 cm deep tunnel draining copious clear serous fluid without odor. Labs showed leukocytosis (WBC 12.6) and elevated CRP (16.3). Wound cultures again grew Enterococcus faecalis and Klebsiella aerogenes.\nAssessment: Non-healing/superficial post-operative surgical wound infection, with concern for a possible deeper infection. Morbid obesity and tobacco use were also noted.\nPlan: The patient declined ER evaluation for a CT scan and IV antibiotics due to billing concerns. The wound was packed with iodoform. Oral antibiotics (Levaquin, Augmentin) were prescribed. Multiple medications including oxycodone and naproxen were discontinued. The case was discussed with the surgeon and wound care clinic, with follow-up scheduled.",
