        "facility": "Avita Health System",
        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back, Abdomen",
        "medicineType": "Orthopedic Surgery",
        "recordType": "Operative Report",
        "summary": "First encounter (9/26/2025, Surgery): The patient, who had previously undergone a two-level total disc arthroplasty at L4-5 and L5-S1, developed a postoperative seroma that began draining at the base of his pannus, with cultures positive for methicillin sensitive staph aureus; he was treated with antibiotics and a wound VAC but subsequently developed a localized fluid collection and increased white count, prompting operative intervention for incision and drainage of the abdominal wall fluid collection, local debridement of subcutaneous fat, and placement of a wound VAC, with minimal blood loss and no complications noted. The operative findings confirmed no communication from the subcutaneous tissue to the musculature or spine, and the patient was transferred to the postoperative unit in stable condition. Second encounter (9/30/2025, Nursing Discharge): The patient was educated on discharge instructions, follow-up appointments, and medication changes, denied any questions, and was discharged with all belongings and home medications after removal of the peripheral IV, tolerating the procedure well and with unchanged assessment at discharge.",
