        "medicineType": "Occupational Therapy",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported back pain and discomfort following recent spine surgery and was admitted for wound infection after surgery; he denied any concerns with self-care since the previous spine surgery and during this admission, stating his fianc\u00e9e was able to assist him at home as needed. He denied any questions or concerns with activities of daily living (ADLs) since the recent admission with wound vac and denied the need for further occupational therapy (OT) assessment with ADLs. Objective: The patient had spinal precautions in place (no lifting &gt;5-10 lbs, no bending/twisting except for self-care purposes, wound vac in place), was independent with all ADLs prior to admission, and was able to perform ADL tasks with strength within functional limits in both upper extremities. He demonstrated appropriate cognitive status, orientation, and safety awareness, and followed all commands without difficulty. Transfers (sit-to-stand, stand-to-sit, bed-to-chair) were performed with modified independence and strength. The patient was able to self-manage his wound vac and received education on wound care and precautions. Assessment: The patient was admitted for wound infection after surgery, with a past medical history of benign essential hypertension and a surgical history including lumbar disc arthroplasty (single and second interspace), fluoroscopy, and injection procedures, as well as left sacroiliac arthrography, lumbar/sacral epidural injection, foot surgery (right), and hernia repair. Plan: No acute OT therapy was warranted as the patient denied any OT needs; OT was to sign off at the patient's request, and the patient planned to continue with physical therapy only during admission.",
