        "medicineType": "Infectious Disease",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported ongoing drainage from the anterior incisional wound following L4/L5 and L5/S1 disc arthroplasty surgery on 08/11/2025, with malodorous drainage developing near the periumbilical incision site on 09/02; cultures at that time showed Klebsiella and Enterococcus, and the patient was given doxycycline from 08/17 to 09/05 for postoperative drainage, with a wound VAC ordered and placed. On 09/05, the patient was referred to Infectious Disease Clinic due to ongoing drainage and complained of mild postoperative anterior and posterior incision pain with radiation down his left leg, but no documented fevers; laboratory workup and abdominopelvic CT were ordered for concern of deeper infection, and the patient was started on Levaquin and Augmentin based on culture results. The patient did not return for follow-up until 09/22, at which time he had not received the CT scan due to insurance issues; he was recommended to go to the ER for a stat CT but deferred, and antibiotics were continued with Flagyl added due to ongoing malodorous drainage, with wound depth measured at approximately 9 cm. On 09/24, the patient went to the ER for CT imaging, which showed a suspected postoperative abscess, and was subsequently referred for hospital admission on 09/26 for surgical evaluation and drainage. The patient denied fevers, chills, sweats, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, respiratory, cardiac, or urological symptoms, but continued to complain of right leg pain radiating from his lower back and some anterior and posterior pain; wound VAC remained in place and he was tolerating IV antibiotics (Zosyn and Levaquin), with midline placement planned.\nObjective: Physical exam revealed the patient was alert, oriented, afebrile, and in no distress, with normal cardiovascular, respiratory, gastrointestinal, and neurologic findings; low anterior abdominal wounds with postoperative VAC were noted, and no suprapubic tenderness, cyanosis, clubbing, or lower extremity cellulitis was present. Laboratory results showed persistent leukocytosis (WBC 12.5-16.6), mild normocytic anemia (Hgb 12.5-14.2, Hct 38.1-43.8), and elevated glucose (104-198 mg/dL), with wound cultures positive for Enterococcus faecalis (susceptible to ampicillin, gentamicin, penicillin G, streptomycin, vancomycin) and Klebsiella aerogenes (resistant to amoxicillin/clavulanic acid and cefazolin, susceptible to cefepime, ceftazidime, ceftriaxone, ertapenem, gentamicin, levofloxacin, imipenem, vancomycin). Imaging on 09/24 revealed a rim-enhancing focal fluid collection measuring approximately 5.5 x 2.2 x 4.3 cm, consistent with a postoperative abscess.\nAssessment: The patient was diagnosed with a postoperative anterior wound infection after lumbar anterior approach surgery, with persistent drainage, abscess formation, and leukocytosis despite oral antibiotics; wound cultures confirmed Enterococcus and Klebsiella, and surgical debridement was performed on 09/26, with no deeper tracking outside the subcutaneous area per operative note.\nPlan: IV antibiotics (Zosyn and Levaquin) were initiated, with midline placement planned and social work consult for outpatient IV antibiotics post-discharge; the patient was advised to keep the line clean, avoid heavy lifting, and not get it wet, and was recommended outpatient management for elevated glucose and weight reduction, with probiotics prescribed while on IV antibiotics.",
