        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented with a postoperative anterior wound infection following lumbar anterior approach disc arthroplasty at L4/L5 and L5/S1 performed on 8/11/2025, with ongoing malodorous drainage from the periumbilical incision site beginning 9/2/2025 and associated right leg pain radiating from the lower back, but denied fevers, chills, sweats, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, respiratory, cardiac, or urological symptoms, and reported only occasional alcohol use and daily cigarette smoking. The patient had a history of degenerative joint disease, disc herniation, hypertension, obesity, and tobacco use, with no history of substance abuse. The patient was initially managed with oral doxycycline, then Levaquin and Augmentin based on wound cultures showing Enterococcus and Klebsiella aerogenes, but continued to have drainage and leukocytosis, and experienced delays in outpatient follow-up due to workman's compensation insurance issues. Objective: On 9/24/2025, CT imaging revealed a rim-enhancing focal fluid collection (5.5 x 2.2 x 4.3 cm) consistent with a postoperative abscess, and the patient was admitted on 9/26/2025 for surgical evaluation and underwent incision and drainage with wound VAC placement, with intraoperative findings showing no deeper tracking outside the subcutaneous tissue. Physical exam during admission was notable for a clean, healing wound with wound VAC, no signs of systemic infection, and normal cardiopulmonary, abdominal, and neurologic findings. Laboratory studies showed persistent leukocytosis (WBC up to 16.6), mild normocytic anemia (Hgb nadir 12.5), and elevated inflammatory markers (CRP up to 23, ESR up to 36), all trending downward with treatment; renal and hepatic function remained stable and within normal limits. Wound cultures grew Enterococcus faecalis (ampicillin susceptible) and Klebsiella aerogenes (resistant to amoxicillin/clavulanic acid and cefazolin, but susceptible to cefepime, ceftazidime, ceftriaxone, ertapenem, gentamicin, and vancomycin). Assessment: The patient was diagnosed with postoperative wound infection and abscess after lumbar disc arthroplasty, with contributing factors including obesity, tobacco use, and delayed follow-up due to insurance barriers. Plan: The patient underwent surgical debridement and wound VAC placement, was transitioned from oral to IV antibiotics (Zosyn, Levaquin, then ertapenem and amoxicillin due to discharge planning and insurance issues), and was discharged with a midline for outpatient IV antibiotics, wound VAC management, and close follow-up with neurosurgery, wound care, and infectious disease; work restrictions included keeping the midline dry and avoiding heavy lifting, and social work coordinated home health and prescription needs.",
