        "summary": "Subjective: A 34-year-old male with hypertension and obesity presented with a postoperative wound infection after a two-level anterior total disc arthroplasty (L4-S1) on 08/11/2025. He developed a seroma that cultured MSSA, had a wound VAC, and subsequently developed right flank pain and a new fluid collection below the umbilicus. He denied fevers, chills, or night sweats but reported low back pain. Pain was rated 3/10 at rest and 6/10 with activity. He was a current smoker (1 pack/day).\nObjective: Vitals showed BP 146/60 and BMI 47.8. An abdominal exam revealed a 15 cm midline scar with an 8 mm opening at the inferior pole, tracking 8-10 cm with clear yellow serous drainage. Labs showed leukocytosis (WBC 14.5 and 16.6), mild anemia, and elevated neutrophils. Physical therapy evaluation found modified independence for mobility but required supervision for balance and gait, ambulating 75 feet. Impairments were noted in strength, balance, transfers, and endurance.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included postoperative wound infection with MSSA seroma, a new loculated abdominal wall fluid collection, hypertension, and obesity.\nPlan: Operative drainage of the fluid collection with debridement and wound VAC placement was scheduled. Antibiotics (Unasyn, Levaquin) and pain medication (Norco) were continued. Physical therapy was initiated for balance, gait, strengthening, and transfer training.",
