        "medicineType": "Infectious Disease",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient, who had an L4-S1 disc arthroplasty on 08/11/2025, developed a postoperative wound complication with malodorous drainage starting 09/02/2025. Wound cultures showed Klebsiella and Enterococcus. He was treated with various antibiotics but continued to have drainage. The patient was noted to be noncompliant with follow-up recommendations, delaying a CT scan due to insurance issues. He presented to the ER on 09/24/2025, and a CT revealed a 5.5 x 2.2 x 4.3 cm fluid collection, suspected to be an abscess, leading to his admission. He still complained of right leg pain radiating from his lower back but denied systemic symptoms.\nObjective: The patient was obese and afebrile with a wound VAC in place on his lower abdomen. Labs showed persistent leukocytosis (WBC up to 16.6) and elevated inflammatory markers (CRP up to 23), though trends were improving. Cultures confirmed Enterococcus faecalis and Klebsiella aerogenes.\nAssessment: Postoperative anterior wound infection with abscess formation following lumbar disc arthroplasty, with causative organisms identified as Enterococcus faecalis and Klebsiella aerogenes. Other diagnoses included leukocytosis and mild anemia.\nPlan: The patient underwent surgical debridement and wound VAC placement, which found no infection deeper than the subcutaneous tissue. IV antibiotics (Zosyn and Levaquin, later changed to ertapenem and amoxicillin) were administered via a midline catheter. The patient was discharged with instructions to avoid heavy lifting and to follow up with neurosurgery, wound care, and infectious disease.",
