        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for evaluation of a postoperative wound infection following L4/L5 and L5/S1 disc arthroplasty on 08/11/2025. He developed malodorous drainage from the periumbilical incision site on 09/02/2025, with cultures showing Klebsiella and Enterococcus. Neurosurgery prescribed doxycycline and ordered a wound VAC. He was referred to Infectious Disease on 09/05 but did not follow up until 09/22 due to insurance issues with a recommended CT scan. At the 09/22 visit, the wound depth was 9 cm, and Flagyl was added to his antibiotic regimen. A CT scan on 09/24 revealed a suspected abscess, leading to admission on 09/26 for surgical evaluation. The patient continued to complain of right leg pain radiating from his lower back but denied fevers, chills, nausea, or vomiting.\nObjective: The patient was alert, oriented, and afebrile. His abdomen was soft with a wound VAC over the low anterior abdominal wounds. Labs showed persistent leukocytosis (WBC 14.6) and mild normocytic anemia (Hgb 13.0). Inflammatory markers were elevated (CRP 16, 23; ESR 36, 31). A CT scan showed a 5.5 x 2.2 x 4.3 cm rim-enhancing fluid collection consistent with an abscess. Wound cultures grew Enterococcus faecalis and Klebsiella aerogenes.\nAssessment: Postoperative anterior wound infection with abscess formation after lumbar disc arthroplasty, with persistent signs of infection despite oral antibiotics. Past medical history was significant for degenerative joint disease, disc herniation, tobacco use, and obesity.\nPlan: The patient underwent surgical debridement on 09/26, which did not reveal tracking outside the subcutaneous area; a wound VAC was placed. IV antibiotics (Zosyn and Levaquin) were initiated. A social work consult was placed to arrange for outpatient IV antibiotic therapy post-discharge. Probiotics and outpatient management for elevated glucose were also recommended.",
