        "summary": "First encounter (Physical Therapy, 09/27/2025): The patient reported pain and discomfort in the back and right hip, with pain rated as 3/10 at rest and with activity according to the Defense and Veterans Pain Rating Scale. The patient ambulated 350 feet without assistive device, demonstrating slowed gait pace, and was instructed in standing lower extremity exercises in all functional planes.\n\nSecond encounter (Nephrology, 09/27/2025): The patient, a 34-year-old male with a history of hypertension, was admitted for postoperative wound infection following incision and drainage of an abdominal wall fluid collection, wound debridement of subcutaneous fat, and placement of a wound VAC after failing outpatient antibiotics. The patient was continued on zosyn, levaquin, norco, and lisinopril for hypertension, with serial CBC monitoring for chronic normocytic anemia. Review of systems was negative for fever, chills, headache, chest pain, cough, diarrhea, constipation, seizure, loss of consciousness, bleeding, or bruising. Physical exam documented weights ranging from 191.7 kg to 195.5 kg over three encounters. Laboratory studies revealed leukocytosis (WBC 14.5-16.6, ref 3.6-11.0), mild normocytic anemia (Hgb 13.0-13.6, ref 14.0-18.0; Hct 39.0-41.8, ref 42.0-52.0), and elevated glucose (168-198 mg/dL, ref 70-100), with normal renal function (creatinine 0.80-1.00, ref 0.7-1.2) and electrolytes. Blood cultures showed no growth after three days.",
