        "summary": "The patient was hospitalized for a wound infection that developed after a two-level anterior lumbar disc arthroplasty from 08/11/2025. The post-operative course was complicated by a seroma, an MSSA infection, and a new fluid collection, which necessitated incision and drainage, debridement, and placement of a wound vac.\n\nSubjective: During the hospitalization, the patient reported ongoing pain in the abdomen and back, with pain levels fluctuating between 3/10 and 9/10. He noted tingling in his left lower extremity. Despite spinal precautions (no lifting &gt;5-10 lbs, no bending/twisting), he reported being independent with self-care.\n\nObjective: A wound vac was in place over the midline lower abdominal incision; the site was documented as clean and dry, though visualization was limited by the dressing. Neurological checks were consistently normal, and assessments for delirium were negative. SOFA scores were consistently zero, indicating no organ dysfunction. Mild weakness was noted in hand grip and ankle strength. Social screening identified concerns with food insecurity, housing, and transportation.\n\nAssessment: Post-operative wound infection management.\n\nPlan: Physical therapy was provided for decreased functional mobility, including balance, gait, and transfer training. Occupational therapy was discontinued at the patient's request. The patient was discharged home in good to fair condition with instructions for home care, including continued IV vancomycin at an outpatient infusion clinic. Extensive patient education was provided on pain management, activity, and when to seek medical care.",
