        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for ongoing wound care after an incision and drainage of an abscess and wound VAC placement on 09/26/2025. He reported doing well with minimal discomfort or drainage from the wound VAC. His appetite was good and activity tolerance was almost normal, though he noted back pain. Pain related to the abdominal wound was rated 4/10 (mild) at rest and 8/10 (severe) with activity, described as constant and sharp, aggravated by movement, and relieved by opioids.\nObjective: Vitals were stable. Examination revealed a large, surgically created abdominal wound measuring 12.2 x 8.1 x 6.8 cm. The wound bed showed nearly 100% beefy red granulation tissue with some visible fascia and undermining from 3:00 to 6:00 o'clock. There was a moderate amount of brown, serous drainage but no redness, induration, or odor. Tunneling was noted at 2 cm at 6 o'clock and 6 cm at 3 o'clock.\nAssessment: Non-healing surgical wound of the abdomen, subsequent encounter.\nPlan: The patient was to continue IV antibiotics and negative pressure wound therapy at 125 mmHg. Dressing changes were scheduled twice weekly, with the next follow-up scheduled for 10/13. Active medications included lisinopril, a nicotine patch, phentermine, hydrocodone-acetaminophen, and methocarbamol.",
