        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for evaluation of lumbar spine complaints two months after a two-level total disc arthroplasty (TDA) at L4-5 and L5-S1 on 08/11/2025. He reported severe, debilitating low back pain with a VAS of 9-10/10 at its worst, difficulty transitioning from sitting to standing, and trouble standing up straight. His postoperative course was complicated by a wound infection, which required an incision and drainage on 09/26/2025 and was being treated with a wound VAC. He reported a history of hypertension, morbid obesity, and smoking one pack of cigarettes per day.\nObjective: Physical examination revealed significant paraspinous lumbar pain with palpation. A CT scan from 09/24/2025 showed no significant postoperative complications related to the TDA, though beam-hardening artifact was noted. Lumbar spine X-rays from the same date also showed no gross postoperative complications on flexion-extension views.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included intervertebral disc displacement in the lumbar and lumbosacral regions, sacroiliitis, and lower back strain.\nPlan: An MRI of the lumbar spine with and without contrast was ordered to rule out infection around the hardware. New lumbar spine X-rays were also ordered. A referral was made for physical therapy, pending MRI results. The patient was to follow up after the imaging.",
