        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for a follow-up wound check after an incision and drainage (I&amp;D) of an abdominal abscess. He reported doing well overall, with no issues managing the wound VAC, which remained sealed. He noted moderate drainage with an odor developing about two days after dressing changes. The patient denied fevers or pain in the wound itself, except during sponge removal. He reported burning, shooting back pain rated 3/10, a rash around the wound, and nearly normal activity tolerance.\nObjective: The patient was alert, oriented, and in no acute distress. The large surgical abdominal wound had an odor but no redness or induration. The wound bed showed mostly beefy red granulation tissue with some green slough. A recurring fungal-appearing rash was noted around the wound. The wound measured 8 cm x 3 cm x 3 cm with a 3 cm deep tunnel at the 3 o'clock position. Vital signs were stable.\nAssessment: Non-healing surgical wound, subsequent encounter. Candidosis of the skin.\nPlan: The wound VAC was held. The wound was packed with Vashe-soaked gauze, rinsed, and then packed with Aquacel AG. A wound culture was collected. The patient was instructed to apply over-the-counter antifungal cream to the rash. Follow-up was scheduled for the next day for a dressing change. Cultures later showed no growth.",
