        "bodyPart": "Abdomen, Lower Back, Back, Skin",
        "medicineType": "Wound Care",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for ongoing care of a non-healing surgical wound from an incision and drainage of an abscess on September 26th. He tolerated the PICO device well but noted irritation from the tape on his right lower abdomen, where there was an open wound. He reported minimal drainage with a slight odor and some itching. He also complained of left lower back pain, rated 3/10 at rest and 6/10 with activity, described as constant, burning, and shooting. The pain was aggravated by activity and relieved by rest and over-the-counter medications. The patient was advised to contact his attorney regarding an expired Medco 14 form for time off, as the provider was unable to complete it.\nObjective: The patient was obese but in no acute distress. The abdominal wound showed some improvement, appearing smaller with some yellow slough on a beefy red wound bed. There was a fungal odor and slightly thicker drainage, but no redness. The midline wound was debrided, measuring 7.7 cm x 1.8 cm x 0.7 cm before and 7.5 cm x 1.7 cm x 0.7 cm after debridement.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included non-healing surgical wound, L5-S1 disc herniation, and candidosis of the skin with indications of a fungal infection in the midline wound.\nPlan: The PICO device was held. The patient was instructed to wash the wound twice weekly, apply Prisma daily, and apply clotrimazole cream to the periwound twice daily, covering with a dry dressing. A follow-up was scheduled in one week.",
        "pdfLink": "pdf",
        "painScore": 3,
        "billingAmount": 4800.0,
        "severity": "critical",
        "isPreCrash": false,
