        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for management of injuries sustained on 04/12/2023 when another semi cut him off, causing him to hit a trailer. He reported moderate, shooting pain in his lower back and right foot, rated 5/10, which was aggravated by lifting, bending, twisting, and walking. He noted improvement with surgery, rest, and Tylenol. He was unemployed and receiving worker's compensation after being dismissed from his prior occupational health provider for a missed appointment.\nObjective: The patient appeared healthy and moved without difficulty. The back exhibited pain on motion and limited range of motion. His right ankle and lower legs showed normal strength and range of motion, but he had pain with certain movements bilaterally, attributed to nerve issues.\nAssessment: Unspecified injury to L4 level of the lumbar spinal cord; stress fracture of the right ankle (healed post-surgery); pain in right leg; and pain in left leg. The patient had undergone L4-L5 back surgery and his surgeon believed the bilateral leg nerve pain was related to the back issue.\nPlan: The patient was advised on pain management with ice, heat, ibuprofen, and acetaminophen. Light duty work restrictions were assigned, prohibiting heavy lifting, carrying, pushing, or pulling. A follow-up was scheduled in three weeks.",
