        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported frustration with ongoing pain and was apprehensive about starting medications that might make him feel 'out of it.' He stated that NSAIDs and lidocaine patches provided no help, and he experienced side effects from cyclobenzaprine and gabapentin. He was optimistic about his recent weight loss.\nObjective: The patient was alert and oriented, in moderate distress, and wearing a back brace. Examination revealed point tenderness in the middle of the left and right low back, painful range of motion, and an antalgic gait. Neurologic exam showed normal sensory and motor function.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included acute lumbar myofascial strain, lumbar disc herniation (M51.26), and sacroiliitis (M46.1).\nPlan: The patient was started on Lyrica with a titration schedule. He was advised he could continue Tylenol and was counseled on using ice and heat. Work restrictions were assigned, noting he was 'basically unable to work at this time due to his pain.' A follow-up was scheduled in three weeks to assess his response to Lyrica.",
