        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient, a 32-year-old male, was referred for evaluation of his right foot and ankle after a semi truck accident on 04/12/2023, in which another semi pulled out in front of him and he struck that vehicle; he refused initial care and transport at the scene but was later taken by his girlfriend to the emergency room, where he was given crutches but received no other treatment, and subsequently saw Occupational Health on 04/18/2023, where he was recommended an ACE wrap, prescribed Norco, and advised Aleve, which he took intermittently; he reported difficulty using crutches due to his size, denied any prior issues with his foot or ankle, and had not returned to work since the incident, with no complaints on the left side. He is a moderate cigarette smoker (10-19 cigarettes/day) and drinks alcohol monthly or less, with a negative alcohol screen. He takes lisinopril for hypertension and has a history of hernia repair. Review of systems was negative for constitutional, ENT, respiratory, cardiovascular, gastrointestinal, genitourinary, skin, and neurologic symptoms, with musculoskeletal complaints as detailed in the HPI.\n\nObjective: On exam, he was noted to have super obesity (BMI 50.92, weight 463.6 lbs, height 6'8\") and wore slip-on sandals due to pain; right ankle exam showed maximal tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament, minimal to moderate tenderness over the navicular site, and a little tenderness over the anterior ankle, with no significant swelling, no medial tenderness, and no forefoot or midfoot tenderness; range of motion was preserved, and left foot/ankle exam was unremarkable with negative anterior drawer and talar tilt tests; knee exam was benign. X-rays of the right ankle showed anterior spurring off the distal tibia (possible early arthritis) and a navicular avulsion fracture, while right foot x-rays showed no instability or other significant findings.\n\nAssessment: The patient was diagnosed with a sprain of the anterior talofibular ligament of the right ankle (initial encounter), right foot navicular avulsion fracture with lateral ankle sprain, status post work injury, and super obesity.\n\nPlan: Treatment included starting Meloxicam 15 mg daily, recommending and providing a fracture boot for immobilization due to inability to use crutches and ongoing pain, counseling on the dangers of tobacco use, and BMI management; follow-up was planned in two weeks, and the patient was restricted from driving but could participate in light duty if transportation was arranged; his healing potential was described as very good, and a follow-up letter was sent to the referring provider.",
