        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up regarding the 04/12/2023 work-related motor vehicle accident, where another semi pulled in front of his truck, causing a collision at approximately 30-35 mph. He sustained injuries to his left shoulder, low back, and right leg, knee, and ankle. He initially refused ambulance transport but was later taken to the ER by his wife due to significant pain. Pain was rated 3-4/10 in the left shoulder, 5-6/10 in the low back, 7-8/10 in the right knee, and 9-10/10 in the right ankle, with numbness and tingling in the right leg. His back pain worsened throughout the day to 7-8/10, radiating to both buttocks and lateral legs, aggravated by sitting and bending, and relieved by lying down. He expressed frustration with the lack of improvement in his back pain six weeks post-injury.\nObjective: Examination revealed tenderness in the lumbar spine radiating bilaterally. Range of motion was painful, and a straight leg raise test caused pain into the buttock. An MRI of the lumbar spine revealed multilevel degenerative changes with focal disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1, causing mild to moderate spinal canal stenosis at L4-L5 and left neural foraminal stenosis at L5-S1. A right ankle x-ray showed a small dorsal navicular fracture.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included acute lumbar myofascial strain, displaced fracture of the right navicular bone, and sprains of the right ankle, right knee, and left shoulder. His knee and shoulder sprains had resolved, but the back condition was unchanged.\nPlan: Physical therapy was approved for the lumbar spine. Gabapentin was increased to 600 mg twice daily, and Tizanidine 4 mg was prescribed as needed. He was to continue follow-up with Dr. Pocos for the navicular fracture.",
