        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: Patient reported ongoing right foot pain from a navicular fracture, with pain increasing to 7-8/10 as the day progressed and worsening with physical therapy, though he was 100% weight-bearing in an ASO brace. He noted improvement in his right knee and left shoulder, with no pain in those areas. He described lower back pain at 8/10, constant and throbbing, radiating down both legs with numbness and tingling, worsened by sitting and walking. He expressed frustration with the lack of improvement in his back pain six weeks post-injury.\nObjective: Physical examination showed no swelling of the right foot, and no pain or swelling in the right knee or left shoulder.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included right foot navicular fracture and lower back strain, with resolved right knee and left shoulder pain. A lumbar spine MRI from 06/07/2023 revealed multilevel degenerative changes, most prominent at L4-L5 and L5-S1. At L4-L5, findings included a posterior disc herniation with a small extruded component causing mild to moderate central canal stenosis. At L5-S1, there was a posterior disc herniation, larger on the left, with an extruded disc extending into the left L5-S1 neural foramen causing foraminal stenosis.\nPlan: Patient was to follow up with Dr. Pocos on June 12, continue using the ASO brace, and take Mobic and Aleve for pain.",
