        "date": "08/03/2023",
        "provider": "Daniel L. Paulson, MD",
        "facility": "Fisher-Titus Medical Center",
        "bodyPart": "Foot, Ankle",
        "medicineType": "Radiology",
        "recordType": "Imaging Report",
        "summary": "Body Part: Right foot and ankle MRI. Impression: No acute osseous findings were identified. There was a chronic appearing deformity of the dorsal navicular, diffuse muscle atrophy especially of the plantar midfoot, and longitudinal split type tearing involving the peroneus brevis tendon. The history noted right foot pain following a motor vehicle accident in April, with continued right lateral foot and ankle pain. MRI technique included routine imaging of the right ankle/hindfoot and forefoot, with comparison to radiographs from 4/12/2023. Additional findings included preserved cartilage in the tibiotalar, subtalar, and midfoot joints, intact major ligaments, mild distal Achilles tendinosis without tear, and intact extensor and medial flexor tendons. There was a physiologic quantity of joint fluid, no evidence for acute fracture, osteochondral lesion, or osteomyelitis, and mild degenerative changes of the first MTP joint without significant effusion. The plantar aponeurosis, sinus tarsi, tarsal tunnel, flexor and extensor tendons, plantar plates, intermetatarsal spaces, and Lisfranc ligament were all within normal limits or intact, and subcutaneous tissues were unremarkable. No other significant abnormality was identified.",
