        "summary": "Body Part: Right foot and ankle MRI. Impression: No acute osseous findings were identified, but there was a chronic appearing deformity of the dorsal navicular, diffuse muscle atrophy especially of the plantar midfoot, and longitudinal split type tearing involving the peroneus brevis tendon. The history noted right foot pain following a motor vehicle accident in April, with continued right lateral foot and ankle pain. MRI technique included routine imaging of the right ankle/hindfoot and forefoot, with comparison to radiographs from 4/12/2023. Cartilage and ligaments appeared preserved and intact, with mild distal Achilles tendinosis but no tear, and extensor and medial flexor tendons intact. The right foot showed mild degenerative changes of the first MTP joint, no significant joint effusion, intact plantar plates, no evidence of Morton's neuroma, and intact Lisfranc ligament. No evidence for acute fracture, osteochondral lesion, or osteomyelitis was found. Subcutaneous tissues were unremarkable, and visualized portions of the plantar aponeurosis and tarsal tunnel were within normal limits. The report was finalized and signed by Paulson MD, Daniel L.",
