        "medicineType": "Occupational Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient reported a work-related injury from a semi-truck collision on 04/12/23, causing injuries to his left shoulder, low back, and right leg/knee/ankle. At this visit, his chief complaints were persistent right ankle and low back pain. He reported right ankle pain at 2-3/10, increasing to 4-5/10 with activity. He described low back pain as 6/10, radiating into the left buttock and down the left leg to his foot, with associated numbness and tingling. He felt best when lying down and was on light duty work (4 hours/day). His medications included meloxicam, tizanidine, gabapentin, and frequent Tylenol. His past medical history was noted for hypertension, morbid obesity, and smoking.\nObjective: The physical examination revealed a limping gait and tenderness across the low back. Lumbar range of motion was limited, with flexion to 70\u00b0, extension to 30\u00b0, and lateral bends to 25\u00b0. Neurological exam showed normal sensory and motor function.\nAssessment: Diagnoses included left shoulder sprain, right knee sprain, right ankle sprain, displaced right foot navicular fracture, acute lumbar myofascial strain, L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniations, and L4-L5 spinal canal stenosis.\nPlan: The patient was restricted from driving a truck or lifting and limited to a 20-hour work week. Medications including meloxicam, tizanidine, and gabapentin were refilled. Follow-up was scheduled with Dr. Dolce and Dr. Blades.",
