        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for evaluation of right foot pain from a work-related motor vehicle accident on 04/12/2023 when a semi pulled out in front of his semi, causing him to rear-end another car. He reported no prior issues with his foot or ankle. He rated his current pain as 8/10, describing persistent significant pain in the lateral ankle despite conservative care including immobilization, physical therapy, and bracing. He noted prior work-related lower back issues affecting his left lower extremity.\nObjective: Examination of the right ankle revealed mild circumferential swelling at the lateral malleolus. There was exquisite tenderness at the watershed region and pain with resisted inversion and eversion at the peroneal brevis tendon. Range of motion was painless and no tendon subluxation was noted. Muscle strength, pulses, and sensation were normal. An MRI showed a longitudinal split tear of the peroneal brevis tendon in the right lateral ankle.\nAssessment: Peroneal tendon rupture, right; synovitis and tenosynovitis of the right ankle and foot.\nPlan: After failing conservative care, surgical repair and tubularization of the peroneal brevis tendon with synovectomy was recommended. The patient was instructed to remain immobilized in his brace. A claim was placed with workers' compensation for surgical approval. Medications included gabapentin, lidocaine, lisinopril, meloxicam, and metoprolol.",
