        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for evaluation of low back pain following a work-related truck accident on 04/12/2023. He reported injuring his left shoulder, low back, right leg, right knee, and right ankle. He had no prior back pain or spinal surgery. Physical therapy from June to July 2023 provided no improvement. He described significant low back pain radiating to both buttocks, the left posterolateral thigh, posterior lateral calf, and into the foot, with the left side being much worse than the right. Symptoms were aggravated by sitting, walking, and bending, and improved by lying down.\nObjective: The patient had difficulty moving from sitting to standing and was using a knee cart after recent right ankle surgery. Examination revealed significant pain over the left sacral sulcus, a positive Fortin finger test bilaterally, and reproduction of left SI joint pain with Faber, leg thrust, and Gaenslen maneuvers. Motor strength was 5/5 in the lower extremities, though right foot testing was deferred. Review of a 06/07/2023 lumbar MRI showed an L4 central/slight left paracentral disc herniation and a left foraminal L5 disc herniation causing moderate foraminal narrowing.\nAssessment: Back strain; Lumbar herniated disc (M51.26); Bilateral sacroiliitis, left greater than right (M46.1).\nPlan: Bilateral hip and pelvis X-rays were ordered. A left SI joint injection and physical therapy for sacroiliitis were recommended, with follow-up after imaging.",
