        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up on his 4/12/23 work injury. By 5/24/23, his right knee pain had improved, but he continued to have 8/10 low back pain radiating down both legs with numbness and tingling. By 7/13/23, he was released from orthopedic care for his foot but still had persistent 6-8/10 back pain radiating into his left buttock and leg. As of this visit, he had undergone a right peroneal brevis tendon repair on 9/28/23 and was cleared for work from a podiatry standpoint. However, he continued to have significant 4-5/10 back pain radiating down both legs with burning and numbness, which worsened through the day. He reported that medications (meloxicam, gabapentin, tizanidine) were not helping and he was no longer taking them.\nObjective: A lumbar spine CT had shown possible L4-L5 herniation and stenosis. A 6/7/23 lumbar MRI revealed focal disc herniations, most prominent at L4-L5 and L5-S1. An 8/3/23 right ankle MRI showed longitudinal split tearing of the peroneus brevis tendon, which was later repaired. Exam revealed tenderness in the low back and left buttock, painful side-bending, and a positive straight leg raise on the left.\nAssessment: Resolved right peroneal tendon injury, shoulder sprain, and knee sprain. Ongoing back pain due to lumbar herniated disc and sacroiliitis.\nPlan: The patient was cleared to return to work from a right foot perspective but with restrictions for his back, including a 4-hour workday, 5 days/week, no truck driving, no lifting, and frequent position changes. He was instructed to get hip x-rays and follow up in two months.",
