        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up of back pain after a left sacroiliac (SI) joint injection on 10/31/2024, which provided 0% pain relief and was deemed ineffective. He reported significant low back and left leg pain, noting a prior caudal epidural was helpful for only about one day. The patient is a current smoker.\nObjective: A 06/07/2023 lumbar spine MRI showed L4-5 and L5-S1 degenerative disc disease with Modic endplate changes and disc herniations. A 10/14/2024 CT scan showed no significant facet arthropathy at those levels. A DEXA scan was normal.\nAssessment: Back strain. The provider concluded the herniated discs were causing the patient's significant low back and leg discomfort.\nPlan: Since injections and physical therapy were ineffective, two-level lumbar total disc arthroplasty (TDA) was considered. However, the patient was advised to first reduce his weight to 300-350 pounds to become a surgical candidate. A referral was made to a weight loss clinic for medication-guided weight loss. The patient was to follow up in six months with new lumbar spine X-rays.",
