        "medicineType": "Bariatric Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented as a new patient for medical weight loss in preparation for spine surgery. He had already lost 35 lbs over two months through a 'carnivore diet' and lifestyle changes. Exercise was limited by back pain, for which he used CBD and marijuana. He was interested in Adipex for affordability reasons. A review of systems was negative for back pain despite his reported limitations.\nObjective: The patient was obese but in no acute distress. Blood pressure was elevated at 141/90. Height was 6'8\", weight was 448 lbs 9.6 oz, and BMI was 49.28 kg/m\u00b2. His weight on 10/31/24 was 480 lbs, confirming a recent 35 lb weight loss. The remainder of the physical exam was unremarkable.\nAssessment: Morbid obesity with BMI of 50.0-59.9, adult.\nPlan: The patient elected to start medically managed weight loss therapy with phentermine after completing labs and a drug screen. A three-month weight loss goal of 22 lbs was established. A nutrition referral was placed, and follow-up was scheduled in one month. Orders were placed for a CBC, comprehensive metabolic panel, TSH, and urine drug screen. His home medications included lisinopril and metoprolol succinate.",
