        "medicineType": "Bariatric Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "First encounter (01/30/2025, Bariatric Medicine): The patient, a 34-year-old male, presented with obesity and reported gradual weight gain, seeking assistance with weight loss. He stated he was down 20 lbs this month, with a goal of 22 lbs, and was meeting preoperative goals for bariatric surgery. He denied side effects such as chest pain, palpitations, headaches, or sleep disturbance, and his blood pressure was at goal. He reported struggling with exercise due to back pain and was encouraged to try chair exercises or pool activities. Dietary changes included eating two meals daily, with encouragement to increase to three meals and meet a calorie goal of 1400-1600. He had previously used phentermine for weight loss and denied heart disease. His weight readings from the last three encounters were 194.2 kg (01/30/25), 203.5 kg (01/02/25), and 217.7 kg (10/31/24), with a current BMI of 47.04. Review of systems was negative for malaise, significant weight loss, fever, headache, swelling, neck lumps, cough, chest pain, abdominal discomfort, skin lesions, or psychological symptoms. Physical exam showed normal general appearance, neurologic status, head, ENT, skin, lungs, heart, abdomen, extremities, and psychiatric findings. The plan included starting/continuing medically managed weight loss therapy with phentermine 37.5 mg daily, dietary and lifestyle counseling, and a three-month weight loss goal of 22 lbs, with follow-up in one month. Labs were reviewed, and the patient was advised to see nutrition as scheduled. At least 20 minutes were spent face-to-face discussing care. Medications at the end of the visit included acetaminophen, lisinopril, metoprolol succinate, and phentermine.\n\nSecond encounter (02/27/2025, Bariatric Medicine): The patient returned for follow-up after completing two months of phentermine, reporting a total weight loss of 23 lbs but only 3 lbs lost in the past month, which he attributed to increased junk food intake. He continued to struggle with exercise due to back pain and was counseled on chair exercises and swimming. He was eating 2-3 times daily with smaller portions but poorer food choices, and a handout was provided. He denied heart disease and reported no significant symptoms on review of systems. His weight was 192.8 kg (425 lb) with a BMI of 47.88. Physical exam findings and review of systems were unremarkable.",
