        "medicineType": "Occupational Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up of a back injury sustained on 4/12/23 when another semi pulled out in front of his truck, causing a collision at 30-35 mph and significant damage to his vehicle; he reported injuries to his left shoulder, low back, right leg, right knee, and right ankle, with pain developing over time after initially refusing ambulance transport and denying head trauma. On 4/18/23, he reported left shoulder pain (3-4/10, sharp, worse with elevation), low back pain (5-6/10, throbbing, intermittent), right knee pain (7-8/10, pounding), and severe right ankle pain (9-10/10, swollen, makes him want to cry); he was not using crutches and had been taking Aleve and Tylenol as needed. He later developed continuous right foot pain (6-7/10), wore a walking boot, and started physical therapy; low back pain was described as dull, radiating, and worsened with activity, with MRI showing herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1 and stenosis. Over subsequent months, he reported persistent low back pain radiating to both legs, numbness, tingling, and cold sensations, with minimal improvement despite PT and medications including meloxicam, gabapentin, and tizanidine. He underwent repair of the peroneus brevis tendon in the right foot and was cleared for work by podiatry, but continued to have significant back pain and was released to light duty, which his employer could not accommodate. He was evaluated by neurosurgery and pain management, with recommendations for sacroiliitis and herniated disc diagnoses and possible injections, but these were initially denied. As of late 2024, he continued to report moderate to severe bilateral low back pain radiating to both buttocks and legs, with numbness, sleep disturbance, and inability to work due to pain; he was only taking Tylenol as needed and was working with a weight loss physician to reduce surgical risk.\n\nObjective: Examination revealed the patient was alert and oriented, in moderate distress, wearing a back brace, with point tenderness in the middle of the left and right low back, painful range of motion, and antalgic gait; neurologic exam showed normal sensory and motor function. MRI of the right ankle showed chronic deformity of the dorsal navicular, diffuse muscle atrophy of the plantar midfoot, and longitudinal split tearing of the peroneus brevis.\n\nAssessment: Diagnoses included acute lumbar myofascial strain, herniated discs at L4-L5 and L5-S1, and sacroiliitis. The patient also had a right foot navicular fracture and peroneus brevis tendon tear.\n\nPlan: The patient was counseled on medication options and started on Lyrica with a titration plan, advised to continue Tylenol and use ice/heat as needed, and was considered unable to work due to pain with a possible return-to-work date in August 2025 if surgery is achieved. He was advised to follow up with neurosurgery and pain management, continue weight loss efforts, and return in three weeks to assess response to Lyrica.",
