        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back, Shoulder, Leg, Knee, Ankle, Foot, Buttocks",
        "medicineType": "Occupational Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up of a back injury sustained on 4/12/23 when another semi pulled out in front of his truck, causing a collision at 30-35 mph and resulting in injuries to his left shoulder, low back, right leg, right knee, and right ankle; he initially refused ambulance transport and reported that pain developed and worsened over time. On 4/18/23, he reported left shoulder pain (3-4/10, sharp, worse with raising arm), low back pain (5-6/10, throbbing, middle/right), right knee pain (7-8/10, pounding), and severe right ankle pain (9-10/10, swollen, makes him want to cry); he was not using crutches and denied bowel/bladder issues. Imaging revealed a small dorsal navicular fracture of the right ankle; he was given crutches and later a walking boot, and started on meloxicam and physical therapy. Over subsequent visits, he reported persistent right foot pain (6-7/10), knee pain (up to 7-8/10 with activity), and low back pain (up to 7-8/10, dull, right-sided, with cold/tingling sensation in legs and feet, improved with lying down, no bowel/bladder dysfunction). By June 2023, MRI showed multilevel degenerative changes with focal disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1; he described back pain as 7-8/10, radiating down both legs, with numbness and tingling, aggravated by sitting and walking, and improved only when lying down. He also reported right foot pain (4-5/10, burning, worse after therapy) and that knee pain had resolved. By July 2023, he was released from the boot but continued to have right foot/ankle pain (2-3/10, burning, worse with driving), and persistent back pain (6-8/10, left-sided, radiating to left buttock and leg, limited ROM, aggravated by sitting, no improvement with PT). MRI of the right ankle in August 2023 showed no acute osseous findings but chronic deformity of the dorsal navicular, diffuse muscle atrophy, and a longitudinal split tear of the peroneus brevis. By late 2023, he underwent surgical repair of the peroneus brevis tendon and was cleared to return to work by podiatry for his foot, but continued to have significant back pain (4-8/10, dull/throbbing, radiating down both legs, burning/numbness, worse with sitting, no bowel/bladder dysfunction). He was seen by pain management and neurosurgery, with recommendations for sacroiliitis and disc herniation diagnoses and injections, but these were denied; he is appealing. He was released to light duty (4 hours/day) but employer had no work for him. By early 2025, neurosurgery recommended surgery but he was considered too high risk due to weight (initially 482 lbs, now 423 lbs); he is working with a weight loss physician and continues to have bilateral low back pain radiating to both buttocks and legs, with numbness, worse from knees to feet, and is unable to work due to pain. He is currently taking acetaminophen, Adipex-P, lisinopril, and metoprolol.\n\nObjective: Imaging on 4/18/23 showed a right ankle dorsal navicular fracture; MRI of the lumbar spine on 6/7/23 revealed multilevel degenerative changes with focal disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1; MRI of the right ankle on 8/3/23 showed chronic deformity of the dorsal navicular, muscle atrophy, and peroneus brevis tear. Physical exam findings included limited range of motion and pain with movement, but no neurological deficits or bowel/bladder dysfunction.\n\nAssessment: The patient was diagnosed with right dorsal navicular fracture, peroneus brevis tendon tear, lumbar disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1, and chronic low back pain with radicular symptoms.\n\nPlan: Treatments included crutches, walking boot, ASO brace, meloxicam, gabapentin, tizanidine, acetaminophen, physical therapy, and surgical repair of the peroneus brevis tendon. Neurosurgery recommended lumbar surgery but this was deferred due to high surgical risk from obesity; the patient is working with a weight loss physician. He was released to light duty but is currently not working due to lack of available work and ongoing pain.",
