        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up of a work-related injury sustained on 4/12/2023 when another semi pulled out in front of his truck, causing a collision at 30-35 mph and resulting in significant damage to his vehicle; he initially refused ambulance transport and later developed pain in the left shoulder, low back, right leg, right knee, and right ankle, with pain radiating to both lower extremities and feet, and reported pain levels ranging from 2-10/10 depending on the body part and activity. He reported using Aleve and Tylenol at home, and later was prescribed meloxicam, gabapentin, and tizanidine, but discontinued these due to lack of efficacy. He also reported using ice and a back brace, and was placed on light duty work restrictions, but was unable to work due to pain and lack of available duties. He described persistent low back pain radiating down both legs, with numbness, tingling, and cold sensations, worse with sitting, walking, or driving, and improved with lying down. He denied bowel or bladder dysfunction throughout. He also reported depression related to his condition.\n\nObjective: Examination consistently revealed moderate distress, slow gait, tenderness across the lower back (left side worse), very limited lumbar range of motion, and use of a back brace. Neurologic exams were generally normal, with no focal deficits documented. Vital signs were stable. MRI of the lumbar spine on 6/7/23 showed multilevel degenerative changes with focal disc herniations at L4-L5 and L5-S1. MRI of the right ankle on 8/3/23 showed chronic deformity of the dorsal navicular, diffuse muscle atrophy, and longitudinal split tearing of the peroneus brevis. \n\nAssessment: Diagnoses included acute lumbar myofascial strain, lumbar disc herniation, and right foot navicular fracture. Sacroiliitis was also suspected and requested for approval but denied by workers' compensation. The patient was noted to be morbidly obese, with weight loss recommended prior to surgical intervention. Depression was also documented.\n\nPlan: The patient was managed with medications including acetaminophen, meloxicam, gabapentin, tizanidine, topiramate, and baclofen, with adjustments made due to efficacy and side effects. He was instructed to use ice/heat as needed, continue back brace use, and follow up with weight loss clinic and neurosurgery; surgery was deferred until further weight loss was achieved. He was restricted from working due to back pain. He was also referred to physical therapy and podiatry for right foot/ankle pain, and completed PT for the ankle and back with minimal improvement. He was fired from his job and remained unemployed due to pain.",
