        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back, Leg, Knee, Ankle, Foot, Shoulder, Buttocks",
        "medicineType": "Occupational Medicine",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up after a work-related motor vehicle accident on 4/12/23, in which another semi pulled out in front of his truck, causing a collision at 30-35 mph and resulting in significant damage to his vehicle; he initially refused ambulance transport and was taken to the ER by his wife, reporting injuries to his left shoulder, low back, right leg, right knee, and right ankle, with pain developing and worsening over time. He reported low back pain radiating to both lower extremities to the feet (7/10), right ankle pain (9-10/10), right knee pain (7-8/10), and left shoulder pain (3-4/10) initially, with pain increasing with movement and activities; he denied bowel or bladder dysfunction and described numbness, tingling, and cold sensations in his legs, with pain worsening throughout the day and with sitting or walking. He later reported improvement in right knee and shoulder pain, but persistent and severe low back pain radiating down both legs, with numbness and tingling, and right foot/ankle pain described as throbbing and burning, worse after therapy; he also described functional limitations, inability to work, and frustration with lack of improvement in back pain despite other injuries improving. He reported ongoing pain and functional limitations through 2024 and 2025, with pain radiating down both legs, numbness, and cold sensations, and described being unable to work due to pain, with failed attempts at medication management and ongoing weight loss efforts as a prerequisite for surgical intervention. He denied bowel or bladder dysfunction and falls, but reported depression and sleep difficulties due to pain.\n\nObjective: Examination revealed the patient was alert and oriented, in moderate distress, with a back brace in place, tenderness across the lower back (left side worse), very limited lumbar range of motion, and slow gait; neurologic exam showed normal sensory and motor function, and no rash was noted. MRI of the lumbar spine on 6/7/23 showed multilevel degenerative changes, most prominent at L4-L5 and L5-S1, with focal disc herniations. MRI of the right ankle on 8/3/23 showed no acute osseous findings, chronic deformity of the dorsal navicular, diffuse muscle atrophy (plantar midfoot), and longitudinal split tearing of the peroneus brevis. Vital signs were stable (BP 120/82).\n\nAssessment: Diagnoses included acute lumbar myofascial strain and herniated lumbar disc, with persistent and severe low back pain radiating to both legs, numbness, and functional impairment; right ankle navicular fracture and peroneus brevis tendon tear; and depression. Sacroiliitis was also suspected and requested for approval by pain management and neurosurgery, but was denied by workers' compensation.\n\nPlan: Treatment included use of a back brace, physical therapy (with limited benefit), medications (meloxicam, gabapentin, tizanidine, acetaminophen, topiramate, baclofen, and previously Lyrica), and ice/heat as needed; Lyrica was discontinued due to overlap with topiramate, and baclofen was started for pain. He was restricted from working due to back pain and was followed monthly by a weight loss clinic, with a goal to reduce weight to 350 lbs or less for surgical eligibility. He was also referred to podiatry and neurosurgery, and had completed physical therapy for his foot and ankle injuries. He was advised to use ice/heat, continue weight loss, and follow up in 6 months. He was released to light duty work for his foot, but his employer had no work available, so he remained off work due to back pain.",
