        "bodyPart": "Back, Lower Back, Leg, Buttocks, Thigh, Lower Leg",
        "medicineType": "Orthopedic",
        "recordType": "Encounter Note",
        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up on lumbar spine complaints, having lost 100 lbs since his last visit, to be evaluated for a potential L4-S1 total disc arthroplasty (TDA). He reported significant low back pain radiating bilaterally to the buttocks, posterolateral thighs, and calves, with a pain score of 8/10. Symptoms worsened with standing, walking, and bending. He also experienced bilateral leg numbness and tingling, severe difficulty rising from a seated position, an inability to stand up straight, and walked bent forward with a limp. He noted he has been unable to work due to his symptoms.\n\nObjective: Physical examination revealed significant difficulty transitioning from sitting to standing and an antalgic gait, walking bent forward. He had notable low back pain in the bilateral paraspinous and gluteal regions. Motor strength was 5/5 in the bilateral lower extremities. Prior imaging included a 06/07/2023 MRI showing L5-S1 degenerative disc disease with moderate disc herniations and a 10/14/2024 CT showing no significant facet arthropathy.\n\nAssessment: The patient was diagnosed with a back strain. Due to his significant weight loss, he was considered a very good candidate for a 2-level TDA at L4-5 and L5-S1. The provider opined that the patient had not reached maximum medical improvement, as a surgical option was still available.\n\nPlan: A new lumbar spine MRI was ordered to confirm surgical candidacy. The patient expressed a desire to proceed with surgery pending the MRI results. Worker's compensation paperwork was completed.",
