        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for wound care following a herniated disc at work on 04/02/2023 and subsequent disc surgery with an anterior approach on 08/11/2025. The surgery was complicated by a postoperative seroma and a non-healing surgical wound with ongoing drainage and a prior infection, which was being managed with antibiotics and negative pressure wound therapy (NPWT). He reported minimal discomfort in the abdominal wound, with no odor, redness, or fever, and noted his back pain was much better. He was allowed to be active with an 8 lb weight restriction.\nObjective: Examination revealed a 4 cm deep open wound in the midline of the very low abdominal region. The wound bed was beefy red and healthy with minimal yellow slough. It undermined all around and had a 7 cm tunnel at the 2:00 position. There was a large to copious amount of serous drainage with no odor, surrounding redness, or tenderness. The patient was alert, oriented, and in no acute distress.\nAssessment: Non-healing surgical wound, subsequent encounter (T81.89XD); Herniation of intervertebral disc between L5 and S1 (M51.27).\nPlan: The wound was packed with iodoform gauze and covered with dry dressings. The patient was instructed to change the top dressing as needed and remove the packing on Friday if it had not already come out. Negative pressure wound therapy was ordered to be set at 125 mmHg, with dressings to be changed three times weekly. The patient was to follow up once the wound vac arrived. No changes were made to his medications, which included amoxicillin-clavulanate, levofloxacin, doxycycline, methocarbamol, and naproxen.",
