        "medicineType": "General Surgery",
        "recordType": "Operative Report",
        "summary": "Preoperative Diagnosis: Strain of muscle, fascia and tendon of lower back, initial encounter. Postoperative Diagnosis: Strain of muscle, fascia and tendon of lower back, initial encounter. Procedure: Incision and drainage of abdominal wall fluid collection (subcutaneous), wound debridement of subcutaneous fat, and placement of wound VAC. The patient, a 34-year-old male with a history of two-level total disc arthroplasty (L4-5, L5-S1), initially did well postoperatively but developed a seroma draining at the base of his pannus, with cultures positive for methicillin sensitive staph aureus, for which he was treated with antibiotics and a wound VAC. He subsequently developed a localized fluid collection and, due to an increased white count, was taken to the operating room for incision and drainage and opening of the tract, with minimal blood loss and no complications. Intraoperatively, no communication was identified from the subcutaneous tissue to the deep layer or dissection tract to the spine.",
