        "medicineType": "Infectious Disease",
        "recordType": "Discharge Summary",
        "summary": "This summary details a hospital admission from 09/26/2025 for a postoperative wound infection.\nSubjective: The patient, with a history of hypertension and prior lumbar spine surgery, presented with a postoperative wound infection following a disc arthroplasty. He reported ongoing right leg pain radiating from his lower back, but denied anterior pain at discharge. The infection involved malodorous drainage from a periumbilical incision, and cultures grew Klebsiella and Enterococcus. The patient noted difficulties in obtaining timely imaging and follow-up due to workers' compensation insurance delays. He denied systemic symptoms like fevers, chills, nausea, or vomiting during hospitalization.\nObjective: On examination, the patient was afebrile and in no distress. His abdomen was soft and non-tender, with a wound VAC in place over the low anterior abdominal wounds. No focal neurologic deficits were observed. Labs showed persistent leukocytosis (WBC 16.6) and mild normocytic anemia. A CT scan of the abdomen/pelvis from 09/24/2025 revealed a 5.5 x 2.2 x 4.3 cm fluid collection consistent with an abscess.\nAssessment: The diagnoses were postoperative wound infection, chronic normocytic anemia, and hypertension.\nPlan: The patient underwent surgical debridement with wound VAC placement. He was treated with IV antibiotics (initially Zosyn and Levaquin, later changed to ertapenem and amoxicillin due to insurance issues). He was discharged home in stable condition with the wound VAC and outpatient IV antibiotics, with prescriptions for Norco, Robaxin, lisinopril, phentermine, nicotine patch, and a probiotic. Follow-up was scheduled with a wound care and infusion clinic.",
