        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for follow-up two months after a two-level total disc arthroplasty (TDA) at L4-S1 on 08-11-2025. He reported significant low back pain, rated up to 9-10/10, with difficulty standing up straight and transitioning from sitting to standing. His ODI was 54. The postoperative course was complicated by a wound infection, requiring an incision and drainage on 09-26-2025 and ongoing treatment with a wound VAC.\nObjective: Physical examination revealed significant paraspinous lumbar pain with palpation and difficulty with posture and movement. A CT scan from 09-24-2025 showed no significant postoperative complication associated with the TDA, although beam-hardening artifact was present. Flexion-extension X-rays from 09-24-2025 also showed no gross postoperative complications.\nAssessment: Lumbar intervertebral disc displacement (M51.26, M51.27), sacroiliitis (M46.1), and lower back strain (S39.012A).\nPlan: An MRI of the lumbar spine with and without contrast was ordered to rule out infection around the hardware. Repeat lumbar spine X-rays and a prescription for physical and/or occupational therapy were also planned. Follow-up was scheduled after imaging to determine further steps, including potential wound closure.",
