        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for wound care follow-up, reporting no pain at the midline abdominal wound with stable drainage and no odor. He noted improvement in a fungal rash after using clotrimazole cream. The right lower abdominal wound was reported as a little sore, while the left was not painful. He felt that keeping the wounds covered was making them too moist. He also reported back pain. He denied fevers and reported a good appetite and fair activity tolerance.\nObjective: The patient was a well-developed, obese male in no acute distress with normal vital signs. The midline abdominal wound measured larger with new tan slough but had no erythema or odor, and the fungal rash was much improved. The right lower abdominal wound was full-thickness with a small amount of drainage, and the left was a smaller, partial-thickness wound with clear drainage; neither had redness.\nAssessment: 1. Non-healing surgical wound. 2. Herniation of intervertebral disc between L5 and S1. 3. Candidosis of skin.\nPlan: The patient was instructed to continue clotrimazole cream, stop Prisma, and use Aquacel Ag with a dry dressing on the midline wound. The lower abdominal wounds could be left open to air unless irritated or draining. Follow-up was scheduled in one week. The provider was unable to complete a Medco 14 form and advised the patient to contact his attorney.",
