        "summary": "Subjective: The patient presented for ongoing wound care for an abdominal abscess, reporting no pain and only a slight odor from the wound. He had a good appetite and fair activity tolerance but continued to have back pain.\nObjective: On examination, the patient was obese and in no acute distress. The main abdominal wound was noted to be smaller and improved, with yellow slough that was easily debrided and moderate serosanguineous exudate. The smaller wounds on the left and right lower abdomen were almost completely closed. The patient was alert and oriented.\nAssessment: The diagnoses included a non-healing surgical wound, herniation of an intervertebral disc between L5 and S1, and cutaneous candidosis.\nPlan: The midline wound was debrided of slough using a sharp technique. Clotrimazole and Prisma were applied, and pico negative pressure therapy was continued. The patient was instructed to reapply the medications with dressing changes at home and follow up in one week.",
